1
00:00:05,320 --> 00:00:06,020
All right.

2
00:00:06,020 --> 00:00:07,960
Let's get started.

3
00:00:07,960 --> 00:00:11,300
So last time, Tatsu talked about scaling laws,

4
00:00:11,300 --> 00:00:14,240
and we're going to take a little break from that

5
00:00:14,240 --> 00:00:17,840
and talk about inference.

6
00:00:17,840 --> 00:00:21,760
So the problem of inference is very simple.

7
00:00:21,760 --> 00:00:24,820
You've trained a model, and you're given a prompt,

8
00:00:24,820 --> 00:00:27,480
and you want to produce the response usually

9
00:00:27,480 --> 00:00:31,600
as accurately and as quickly as you can.

10
00:00:31,600 --> 00:00:35,820
So inference turns out to be only one lecture,

11
00:00:35,820 --> 00:00:39,640
but it's actually of growing importance.

12
00:00:39,640 --> 00:00:42,680
And it shows up in many places.

13
00:00:42,680 --> 00:00:44,580
So clearly, once you've trained a model,

14
00:00:44,580 --> 00:00:45,980
you don't just sit there.

15
00:00:45,980 --> 00:00:48,200
And if you're a researcher, you put

16
00:00:48,200 --> 00:00:50,940
a plot in your paper of here's how my model works,

17
00:00:50,940 --> 00:00:54,300
but for everyone else, you actually want to use this model.

18
00:00:54,300 --> 00:00:56,120
So what does use look like?

19
00:00:56,120 --> 00:00:59,830
It could be chatting with an AI assistant or chatbot,

20
00:00:59,830 --> 00:01:02,670
it could be doing code completion.

21
00:01:02,670 --> 00:01:04,950
These days, agents are very popular,

22
00:01:04,950 --> 00:01:08,030
and that requires inference.

23
00:01:08,030 --> 00:01:12,430
Batch data processing as well.

24
00:01:12,430 --> 00:01:15,790
It's also used for evaluation.

25
00:01:15,790 --> 00:01:19,670
For evaluation that requires generation in particular.

26
00:01:19,670 --> 00:01:21,565
And it's also used inside training.

27
00:01:21,565 --> 00:01:23,190
If you're doing reinforcement learning,

28
00:01:23,190 --> 00:01:25,750
you need to have a model, you generate rollouts,

29
00:01:25,750 --> 00:01:28,950
and you score them, and you update the weights appropriately.

30
00:01:28,950 --> 00:01:32,510
So inference shows up all over the place.

31
00:01:32,510 --> 00:01:38,270
And efficiency, as is the theme of this class,

32
00:01:38,270 --> 00:01:41,230
really matters more than ever here.

33
00:01:41,230 --> 00:01:48,830
And even if you just look at the actual use app of vantage point,

34
00:01:48,830 --> 00:01:50,550
training is a one-time cost.

35
00:01:50,550 --> 00:01:51,690
It could be very expensive.

36
00:01:51,690 --> 00:01:52,770
It is very expensive.

37
00:01:52,770 --> 00:01:55,150
But once you're done with it, that's it.

38
00:01:55,150 --> 00:01:57,900
But inference is a repeated cost.

39
00:01:57,900 --> 00:02:01,660
You kind of incur them every single day.

40
00:02:01,660 --> 00:02:09,060
So OpenAI is estimated to produce 8.6 trillion tokens a day.

41
00:02:09,060 --> 00:02:11,980
And just for reference, DeepSeek-V4,

42
00:02:11,980 --> 00:02:13,560
which came out earlier this year,

43
00:02:13,560 --> 00:02:16,380
was trained on 32 trillion tokens.

44
00:02:16,380 --> 00:02:19,060
So in less than four days, the number of tokens

45
00:02:19,060 --> 00:02:22,380
that OpenAI has to produce, and therefore the compute

46
00:02:22,380 --> 00:02:26,540
is at least DeepSeek-V4.

47
00:02:26,540 --> 00:02:29,620
And of course, even the frontier models

48
00:02:29,620 --> 00:02:31,900
might be trained on more tokens, but still,

49
00:02:31,900 --> 00:02:37,500
inference, the number of tokens generated is going to be higher.

50
00:02:37,500 --> 00:02:40,660
So that's just some perspective.

51
00:02:40,660 --> 00:02:43,860
And moreover, I think the importance of inference

52
00:02:43,860 --> 00:02:48,580
has grown in the last year because once upon a time,

53
00:02:48,580 --> 00:02:50,980
we thought of language models as primarily

54
00:02:50,980 --> 00:02:55,560
as chatbots or assistants, where you put in a prompt,

55
00:02:55,560 --> 00:02:58,650
you get back a response, and presumably,

56
00:02:58,650 --> 00:03:01,130
the goal of the human is to read the response.

57
00:03:01,130 --> 00:03:04,850
But now, as we move more into an agentic world,

58
00:03:04,850 --> 00:03:08,050
what it looks like is that a query goes in,

59
00:03:08,050 --> 00:03:11,230
and the agent is going to do a bunch of stuff.

60
00:03:11,230 --> 00:03:13,470
It's going to think, it's going to reason,

61
00:03:13,470 --> 00:03:15,970
it's going to call some tools, it's going to introspect.

62
00:03:15,970 --> 00:03:18,850
And at the end of the day, it will produce some output

63
00:03:18,850 --> 00:03:20,970
for a human to read.

64
00:03:20,970 --> 00:03:23,570
So most of the tokens that the agent produces

65
00:03:23,570 --> 00:03:27,050
is actually not for reading.

66
00:03:27,050 --> 00:03:29,210
And so you should really think about the number

67
00:03:29,210 --> 00:03:32,490
of tokens generated as really the compute spend.

68
00:03:32,490 --> 00:03:34,110
And there's no limit.

69
00:03:34,110 --> 00:03:36,290
If you have an ambitious enough problem,

70
00:03:36,290 --> 00:03:41,930
you're going to need much compute and a lot of tokens.

71
00:03:41,930 --> 00:03:43,930
So if you were in the chatbot world,

72
00:03:43,930 --> 00:03:47,390
maybe inference after a certain point,

73
00:03:47,390 --> 00:03:51,450
it's fast enough, because humans can only read so fast.

74
00:03:51,450 --> 00:03:56,590
But for an agent, there's no limit to how much a value

75
00:03:56,590 --> 00:04:00,430
you can get out of squeezing more out of inference.

76
00:04:00,430 --> 00:04:03,910
So there's a lot of people doing inference

77
00:04:03,910 --> 00:04:06,310
on the commercial side.

78
00:04:06,310 --> 00:04:08,830
Of course, all the closed API providers

79
00:04:08,830 --> 00:04:11,070
have to serve their models, so inference

80
00:04:11,070 --> 00:04:12,870
is a big deal for them.

81
00:04:12,870 --> 00:04:15,550
And there is also a bunch of providers

82
00:04:15,550 --> 00:04:20,149
serving open-weight models and providing inference as well.

83
00:04:20,149 --> 00:04:21,970
And in the open source community,

84
00:04:21,970 --> 00:04:23,490
there's a bunch of packages.

85
00:04:23,490 --> 00:04:25,670
So vLLM is probably a popular one.

86
00:04:25,670 --> 00:04:28,430
Kind of a go-to.

87
00:04:28,430 --> 00:04:31,590
SGLang is another one that's particularly

88
00:04:31,590 --> 00:04:35,690
good for agentic workloads, but maybe not as popular yet.

89
00:04:35,690 --> 00:04:39,770
And then there's TensorRT from NVIDIA, which is really fast,

90
00:04:39,770 --> 00:04:42,190
but it's more narrow.

91
00:04:42,190 --> 00:04:47,710
And then llama.cpp, if you want to run inference on CPU,

92
00:04:47,710 --> 00:04:50,630
this is a popular package.

93
00:04:50,630 --> 00:04:55,540
So hopefully, I've argued that inference is huge.

94
00:04:55,540 --> 00:05:00,440
If you can make inference twice as fast or even 10% faster,

95
00:05:00,440 --> 00:05:02,460
that is a big deal.

96
00:05:02,460 --> 00:05:05,340
So now, the question is, what does fast mean?

97
00:05:05,340 --> 00:05:08,400
So there's a few metrics that capture the notion of fast.

98
00:05:08,400 --> 00:05:11,700
And they're going to be applicable in different settings

99
00:05:11,700 --> 00:05:13,740
and have different trade-offs.

100
00:05:13,740 --> 00:05:19,580
So one metric of fast is time to first token or TTFT.

101
00:05:19,580 --> 00:05:21,900
This is essentially how long a user waits

102
00:05:21,900 --> 00:05:24,260
before any generation happens.

103
00:05:24,260 --> 00:05:28,700
So you put in a query into ChatGPT,

104
00:05:28,700 --> 00:05:36,740
and some number of milliseconds passes by, and then

105
00:05:36,740 --> 00:05:40,820
from the point the first token comes in, that is the time.

106
00:05:40,820 --> 00:05:43,540
And this is generally useful for interactive applications

107
00:05:43,540 --> 00:05:46,900
because that latency where you're just waiting and doing

108
00:05:46,900 --> 00:05:48,860
nothing is--

109
00:05:48,860 --> 00:05:51,870
the longer that is, the worse the user experience,

110
00:05:51,870 --> 00:05:54,210
and as soon as tokens come in, it doesn't maybe

111
00:05:54,210 --> 00:05:57,690
have to be that fast, because if you're going to read it anyway,

112
00:05:57,690 --> 00:06:00,530
you can't read that fast.

113
00:06:00,530 --> 00:06:03,650
So the second metric is latency.

114
00:06:03,650 --> 00:06:06,510
And this is from the standpoint of an individual user.

115
00:06:06,510 --> 00:06:09,470
How fast are tokens appearing for one query?

116
00:06:09,470 --> 00:06:14,330
This is also important for interactive applications.

117
00:06:14,330 --> 00:06:17,370
So basically, this is how fast the tokens are kind of streaming

118
00:06:17,370 --> 00:06:19,490
through.

119
00:06:19,490 --> 00:06:21,170
And then the second related concept

120
00:06:21,170 --> 00:06:24,570
is throughput, measuring tokens per second,

121
00:06:24,570 --> 00:06:28,410
This is how fast tokens are appearing for many queries.

122
00:06:28,410 --> 00:06:31,930
So latency, or one latency, and throughput

123
00:06:31,930 --> 00:06:34,490
are clearly very related.

124
00:06:34,490 --> 00:06:38,410
It's very kind of aligned. In general,

125
00:06:38,410 --> 00:06:42,250
many interventions will make latency and throughput better.

126
00:06:42,250 --> 00:06:46,930
But as we'll see later, there's actually a trade-off here.

127
00:06:46,930 --> 00:06:49,360
And throughput is useful for if you're doing

128
00:06:49,360 --> 00:06:52,080
a bunch of batch processing.

129
00:06:52,080 --> 00:06:54,560
You have a petabyte of data, and you

130
00:06:54,560 --> 00:06:57,000
want to process it with a language model.

131
00:06:57,000 --> 00:06:59,100
You just want that job to get done.

132
00:06:59,100 --> 00:07:03,240
It doesn't matter if one query is coming in faster, sooner,

133
00:07:03,240 --> 00:07:04,960
or later.

134
00:07:04,960 --> 00:07:06,600
OK.

135
00:07:06,600 --> 00:07:09,360
So what determines the efficiency

136
00:07:09,360 --> 00:07:14,280
by any of these metrics of inference?

137
00:07:14,280 --> 00:07:18,240
So the high-level bit is as follows.

138
00:07:18,240 --> 00:07:21,000
And this is maybe one high-level bit

139
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:26,000
to remember from the lecture: that when you're doing training,

140
00:07:26,000 --> 00:07:28,040
you see all the tokens at once, because

141
00:07:28,040 --> 00:07:30,880
in supervised fine-tuning, you see all the tokens,

142
00:07:30,880 --> 00:07:33,260
and you can parallelize over the sequence.

143
00:07:33,260 --> 00:07:35,200
Remember, think about in the transformer,

144
00:07:35,200 --> 00:07:38,360
the calculation for attention, MLP,

145
00:07:38,360 --> 00:07:40,020
the sequence is just a dimension.

146
00:07:40,020 --> 00:07:42,920
So it's a big tensor that gets multiplied.

147
00:07:42,920 --> 00:07:47,150
And so you essentially process all the tokens at once.

148
00:07:47,150 --> 00:07:50,030
In inference, you can't do that.

149
00:07:50,030 --> 00:07:52,950
You have to generate because of the autoregressive nature

150
00:07:52,950 --> 00:07:54,430
of inference,

151
00:07:54,430 --> 00:07:55,450
tokens sequentially,

152
00:07:55,450 --> 00:07:56,910
one at a time.

153
00:07:56,910 --> 00:07:59,790
So this is the fundamental problem

154
00:07:59,790 --> 00:08:02,870
of why inference is a very different workload

155
00:08:02,870 --> 00:08:05,870
than training, because you can't parallelize across the sequence

156
00:08:05,870 --> 00:08:07,030
dimension.

157
00:08:07,030 --> 00:08:08,850
And therefore, as we'll see later,

158
00:08:08,850 --> 00:08:12,030
it's going to be harder to have high arithmetic intensity

159
00:08:12,030 --> 00:08:15,710
or fully utilize the compute.

160
00:08:15,710 --> 00:08:18,605
OK.

161
00:08:18,605 --> 00:08:19,450
All right.

162
00:08:19,450 --> 00:08:21,370
So let's go on.

163
00:08:21,370 --> 00:08:24,710
So first, I'm going to-- just maybe a preview of what's

164
00:08:24,710 --> 00:08:26,270
in this lecture.

165
00:08:26,270 --> 00:08:31,670
First, I'm going to do some math to understand the arithmetic

166
00:08:31,670 --> 00:08:34,850
intensity and throughput and latency,

167
00:08:34,850 --> 00:08:37,549
how to think about that for a transformer.

168
00:08:37,549 --> 00:08:40,110
And then I'm going to talk about various techniques

169
00:08:40,110 --> 00:08:46,280
to reduce the cost by making the KV cache smaller,

170
00:08:46,280 --> 00:08:48,900
quantizing model pruning, and then

171
00:08:48,900 --> 00:08:51,260
finally, I'll talk about speculative decoding, and then

172
00:08:51,260 --> 00:08:55,460
some kind of practical concerns.

173
00:08:55,460 --> 00:08:56,000
All right.

174
00:08:56,000 --> 00:08:58,917
So this is going to be a bit of a review, mostly

175
00:08:58,917 --> 00:09:00,500
kind of swapping in, making sure we're

176
00:09:00,500 --> 00:09:03,060
on the same page with respect to notation.

177
00:09:03,060 --> 00:09:06,820
Much of this lecture is based on the scaling book from Google

178
00:09:06,820 --> 00:09:09,480
on transformers and inference.

179
00:09:09,480 --> 00:09:11,880
So I do recommend that people go check it out.

180
00:09:11,880 --> 00:09:13,840
It's a very nicely written.

181
00:09:13,840 --> 00:09:22,660
And some of the figures are generously lifted from that book.

182
00:09:22,660 --> 00:09:23,620
OK.

183
00:09:23,620 --> 00:09:27,020
So just to establish a bit of notation

184
00:09:27,020 --> 00:09:30,380
to understand this diagram.

185
00:09:30,380 --> 00:09:33,460
So we're going to use symbols.

186
00:09:33,460 --> 00:09:37,020
Think about it in einops to denote dimensions,

187
00:09:37,020 --> 00:09:38,640
but also they're kind of length.

188
00:09:38,640 --> 00:09:41,540
So B is going to be the batch dimension and also

189
00:09:41,540 --> 00:09:42,893
the number of sequences.

190
00:09:42,893 --> 00:09:44,810
T is going to be the sequence dimension, which

191
00:09:44,810 --> 00:09:46,230
is also the number of tokens.

192
00:09:46,230 --> 00:09:49,770
D is the model dimension, H is the dimension.

193
00:09:49,770 --> 00:09:57,930
So if I'm going to write this, this taking a product between a tensor with dimensions B, T,

194
00:09:57,930 --> 00:10:03,850
and D, and another matrix of D and H, and I produce BTH.

195
00:10:03,850 --> 00:10:06,050
So what is going on here is that there

196
00:10:06,050 --> 00:10:08,490
are some dimensions marked red, which

197
00:10:08,490 --> 00:10:10,290
are going to be contracting.

198
00:10:10,290 --> 00:10:13,210
These dimensions appear in both the operands

199
00:10:13,210 --> 00:10:16,370
and disappear from the results.

200
00:10:16,370 --> 00:10:19,570
And then there's going to be other regular dimensions

201
00:10:19,570 --> 00:10:25,690
in black that appear only in one operand and stay in the result.

202
00:10:25,690 --> 00:10:28,050
So this is the general form.

203
00:10:28,050 --> 00:10:31,730
Another case is if you have blue dimensions, that

204
00:10:31,730 --> 00:10:33,890
means they're attaching dimensions,

205
00:10:33,890 --> 00:10:36,630
and they appear in both, but stay in the result.

206
00:10:36,630 --> 00:10:40,250
They do not get contracted or reduced.

207
00:10:40,250 --> 00:10:40,750
OK.

208
00:10:40,750 --> 00:10:43,220
So the interpretation here, as we'll see later,

209
00:10:43,220 --> 00:10:46,560
is that we have this tensor for every sequence

210
00:10:46,560 --> 00:10:47,540
and token position.

211
00:10:47,540 --> 00:10:53,080
You have a vector and you want to multiply that by a matrix.

212
00:10:53,080 --> 00:10:55,080
And then here, you're basically taking

213
00:10:55,080 --> 00:10:58,900
a bunch of dot products between two tensors.

214
00:10:58,900 --> 00:10:59,400
OK.

215
00:10:59,400 --> 00:11:03,440
So with that in mind, this in all its full glory,

216
00:11:03,440 --> 00:11:08,380
is the description of a transformer block.

217
00:11:08,380 --> 00:11:10,380
So it looks a little bit like a circuit diagram.

218
00:11:10,380 --> 00:11:12,640
There's a lot of details here, but I actually

219
00:11:12,640 --> 00:11:17,600
find this probably the most crisp definition

220
00:11:17,600 --> 00:11:19,480
of what a transformer is.

221
00:11:19,480 --> 00:11:21,420
You see a lot of description of transformer,

222
00:11:21,420 --> 00:11:24,200
and frankly, a lot of them are-- it's

223
00:11:24,200 --> 00:11:26,762
hard to understand what is exactly happening.

224
00:11:26,762 --> 00:11:28,720
This one basically tells you exactly the shapes

225
00:11:28,720 --> 00:11:33,520
of the tensors and allows you to reason about the dependency.

226
00:11:33,520 --> 00:11:35,960
So for transformer block, you take

227
00:11:35,960 --> 00:11:39,300
the X, which is the activations of one layer,

228
00:11:39,300 --> 00:11:41,100
you feed it through a tension, and then

229
00:11:41,100 --> 00:11:44,020
you feed it through MLP.

230
00:11:44,020 --> 00:11:50,860
And just as a kind of a quick refresher,

231
00:11:50,860 --> 00:11:55,900
you projected using a query matrix, a key matrix and a value

232
00:11:55,900 --> 00:12:02,820
matrix, you'll see here that we have

233
00:12:02,820 --> 00:12:04,980
N, which is the number of heads, and H

234
00:12:04,980 --> 00:12:06,700
is the dimension of each head.

235
00:12:06,700 --> 00:12:10,740
So the query matrix is batch times sequence times number

236
00:12:10,740 --> 00:12:13,060
of heads times the head dimension,

237
00:12:13,060 --> 00:12:15,800
and K and V, instead of having N,

238
00:12:15,800 --> 00:12:18,620
we have K, which we'll see later,

239
00:12:18,620 --> 00:12:23,860
which we have a potentially smaller number key value heads.

240
00:12:23,860 --> 00:12:26,820
And then this is the attention operation,

241
00:12:26,820 --> 00:12:29,980
where notice that there's batching dimensions.

242
00:12:29,980 --> 00:12:32,540
So B appears in both.

243
00:12:32,540 --> 00:12:36,620
And we're contracting over the head dimension.

244
00:12:36,620 --> 00:12:40,890
And we'll come back to why this B, B in both,

245
00:12:40,890 --> 00:12:43,410
makes inference hard.

246
00:12:43,410 --> 00:12:45,690
Why attention is kind of a bottleneck there.

247
00:12:45,690 --> 00:12:49,610
So just hold on to that thought.

248
00:12:49,610 --> 00:12:55,170
And the MLP is very simple and straightforward.

249
00:12:55,170 --> 00:12:57,510
You just do some math models.

250
00:12:57,510 --> 00:13:03,090
This is the gating matrix.

251
00:13:03,090 --> 00:13:05,230
This is the upper direction and a down projection.

252
00:13:05,230 --> 00:13:06,470
So thank you guys.

253
00:13:06,470 --> 00:13:07,470
I have implemented this.

254
00:13:07,470 --> 00:13:09,095
So I don't want to belabor it, but just

255
00:13:09,095 --> 00:13:11,370
to swap in the notation.

256
00:13:11,370 --> 00:13:18,050
By convention, we're going to assume that in the MLP,

257
00:13:18,050 --> 00:13:22,450
the MLP up projects the D dimensional, model dimension

258
00:13:22,450 --> 00:13:23,670
into four times that.

259
00:13:23,670 --> 00:13:28,250
So F is always--think about it as 4D whenever you see F.

260
00:13:28,250 --> 00:13:32,150
And then the model dimension gets split across the N heads.

261
00:13:32,150 --> 00:13:35,370
So the model dimension is always equal to number of heads

262
00:13:35,370 --> 00:13:38,000
times the head dimension.

263
00:13:38,000 --> 00:13:43,040
And then the number of heads get split,

264
00:13:43,040 --> 00:13:45,640
in the case of group query attention,

265
00:13:45,640 --> 00:13:51,920
into the number of groups and the number of heads per group.

266
00:13:51,920 --> 00:13:54,760
And finally, we have two variables, S and T,

267
00:13:54,760 --> 00:13:59,160
which both represents the sequence dimension.

268
00:13:59,160 --> 00:14:01,080
And the difference is that S is going

269
00:14:01,080 --> 00:14:05,520
to represent the number of input tokens,

270
00:14:05,520 --> 00:14:08,680
and T is going to represent the number of output tokens.

271
00:14:08,680 --> 00:14:10,720
So in training time, these two are the same,

272
00:14:10,720 --> 00:14:15,560
because we're predicting all the same inputs as outputs.

273
00:14:15,560 --> 00:14:18,000
And inference time, T is going to be 1,

274
00:14:18,000 --> 00:14:19,660
and S is going to be the input.

275
00:14:22,960 --> 00:14:23,960
OK.

276
00:14:23,960 --> 00:14:26,380
So another review for arithmetic intensity.

277
00:14:26,380 --> 00:14:28,520
So I talked about this in the second lecture.

278
00:14:28,520 --> 00:14:29,520
So just as a warm up.

279
00:14:29,520 --> 00:14:32,240
Suppose I'm multiplying two matrices; a B by D

280
00:14:32,240 --> 00:14:34,600
matrix and a D by F matrix.

281
00:14:34,600 --> 00:14:35,990
So intuition.

282
00:14:35,990 --> 00:14:38,390
B is the batch dimension, D is the hidden dimension

283
00:14:38,390 --> 00:14:41,310
or the model dimension, and F is the projection dimension

284
00:14:41,310 --> 00:14:43,470
in the MLP.

285
00:14:43,470 --> 00:14:47,030
So remember, what is the arithmetic intensity?

286
00:14:47,030 --> 00:14:48,990
I have to count flops, and I have

287
00:14:48,990 --> 00:14:52,630
to count the amount of memory moved.

288
00:14:52,630 --> 00:14:56,150
So we can do this as follows.

289
00:14:56,150 --> 00:15:01,110
So what do you have to do to multiply these matrices?

290
00:15:01,110 --> 00:15:04,550
Now, remember, the systems lectures,

291
00:15:04,550 --> 00:15:08,550
you have to read X from HBM.

292
00:15:08,550 --> 00:15:10,990
And if you're storing everything in bf16,

293
00:15:10,990 --> 00:15:13,870
which is the case for inference, we're

294
00:15:13,870 --> 00:15:17,630
going to have 2 times B times D, you're going to read W. That's

295
00:15:17,630 --> 00:15:21,070
going to be another 2 times D times F.

296
00:15:21,070 --> 00:15:22,450
You're going to do the matmul.

297
00:15:22,450 --> 00:15:29,920
So the number of flops is 2 times B times D times F

298
00:15:29,920 --> 00:15:33,180
And then you're going to write it back.

299
00:15:33,180 --> 00:15:33,860
OK?

300
00:15:33,860 --> 00:15:36,160
So every time you read and write,

301
00:15:36,160 --> 00:15:38,880
it's basically the number of entries.

302
00:15:38,880 --> 00:15:42,920
It's kind of a quadratic-like term, and matmuls are cubic.

303
00:15:42,920 --> 00:15:45,530
And remember, this is going to be important

304
00:15:45,530 --> 00:15:47,780
because that's how you're going to get high arithmetic

305
00:15:47,780 --> 00:15:48,900
intensity.

306
00:15:48,900 --> 00:15:53,440
So the total number of flops is the cubic,

307
00:15:53,440 --> 00:15:57,780
and then the bytes transferred is the sum of the reads

308
00:15:57,780 --> 00:15:59,980
and writes.

309
00:15:59,980 --> 00:16:03,140
So remember, arithmetic intensity is how much compute

310
00:16:03,140 --> 00:16:04,920
we do per byte transfer.

311
00:16:04,920 --> 00:16:06,260
And we want this to be high.

312
00:16:06,260 --> 00:16:13,020
So the intensity is going to be equal to flops

313
00:16:13,020 --> 00:16:14,775
over bytes transferred.

314
00:16:14,775 --> 00:16:16,900
And I'm representing all these things symbolically,

315
00:16:16,900 --> 00:16:20,540
because it will be easier to see and work

316
00:16:20,540 --> 00:16:23,780
with rather than numbers.

317
00:16:23,780 --> 00:16:27,340
One simplification we can make

318
00:16:27,340 --> 00:16:29,820
is that suppose that the batch dimension here

319
00:16:29,820 --> 00:16:34,730
is much less than D and F, then we can simplify this.

320
00:16:34,730 --> 00:16:38,490
So in particular, what this is doing is saying D equals C

321
00:16:38,490 --> 00:16:42,030
times D and F equals C times B and let C goes to infinity,

322
00:16:42,030 --> 00:16:45,570
so both D and F are really large and B is smaller,

323
00:16:45,570 --> 00:16:51,590
and this just reduces to the intensity of B.

324
00:16:51,590 --> 00:16:55,330
So the upshot of that is that remember

325
00:16:55,330 --> 00:16:59,490
when we did arithmetic intensity for matmul,

326
00:16:59,490 --> 00:17:04,290
it was something like N/3.

327
00:17:04,290 --> 00:17:07,130
And this is the kind of analog, where

328
00:17:07,130 --> 00:17:12,430
instead of just having square matrices,

329
00:17:12,430 --> 00:17:14,829
we have non-square matrices.

330
00:17:14,829 --> 00:17:15,329
OK.

331
00:17:15,329 --> 00:17:18,930
So just to compare, so the accelerator intensity

332
00:17:18,930 --> 00:17:24,410
of a hardware is you look at the flops per second

333
00:17:24,410 --> 00:17:28,490
in the spec sheet, you look at how much memory bandwidth, how

334
00:17:28,490 --> 00:17:31,920
fast memory gets moved between HBM, you divide,

335
00:17:31,920 --> 00:17:35,160
and that is the accelerated intensity.

336
00:17:35,160 --> 00:17:40,240
And now, you compare your computations intensity

337
00:17:40,240 --> 00:17:42,400
with your accelerator intensity.

338
00:17:42,400 --> 00:17:47,540
And if it's greater then your compute bound, which is good,

339
00:17:47,540 --> 00:17:50,480
if you're less, then you're memory bound, and that's bad.

340
00:17:50,480 --> 00:17:54,920
So in this case, for H100, for this particular matmul

341
00:17:54,920 --> 00:17:58,760
operation, you're compute bound if the batch

342
00:17:58,760 --> 00:18:03,840
size is greater than 295.

343
00:18:03,840 --> 00:18:06,000
So here's an extreme case.

344
00:18:06,000 --> 00:18:11,160
So suppose you just have one example.

345
00:18:11,160 --> 00:18:13,320
What happens is you have one example, then

346
00:18:13,320 --> 00:18:16,320
your arithmetic intensity is going to be one.

347
00:18:16,320 --> 00:18:21,040
And this is going to be memory bound.

348
00:18:21,040 --> 00:18:23,420
And you can see what's going on here.

349
00:18:23,420 --> 00:18:27,140
You're reading this DF matrix, but B is only one.

350
00:18:27,140 --> 00:18:31,960
So you're effectively doing only 2 times D times F flops.

351
00:18:34,690 --> 00:18:37,020
And this is basically the workload

352
00:18:37,020 --> 00:18:38,720
that you'll see in inference.

353
00:18:38,720 --> 00:18:40,400
You don't get these full matrices,

354
00:18:40,400 --> 00:18:45,680
you get these very thin matrices or tensors.

355
00:18:45,680 --> 00:18:46,180
OK.

356
00:18:46,180 --> 00:18:50,100
Let me stop there in case there's any questions.

357
00:18:50,100 --> 00:18:51,140
Yeah.

358
00:18:51,140 --> 00:18:56,512
In the transformer slide, I just want to confirm [INAUDIBLE].

359
00:19:03,409 --> 00:19:09,468
You said, across [INAUDIBLE] can search across [INAUDIBLE]?

360
00:19:13,340 --> 00:19:17,960
Yeah, should that be across tables instead of cubes?

361
00:19:22,180 --> 00:19:22,960
Let's see.

362
00:19:22,960 --> 00:19:31,790
So the number of groups here is—wait, I'm just making sure.

363
00:19:31,790 --> 00:19:36,490
So K is the number of key value heads.

364
00:19:36,490 --> 00:19:45,150
And so you have g per KV head.

365
00:19:48,010 --> 00:19:54,255
My understanding is [INAUDIBLE] each key path will have one

366
00:19:54,255 --> 00:19:59,010
[INAUDIBLE] correspond to it.

367
00:19:59,010 --> 00:20:06,450
And [INAUDIBLE] then g is the number of [INAUDIBLE] p.

368
00:20:06,450 --> 00:20:08,290
That means we have k.

369
00:20:08,290 --> 00:20:09,130
That's my question.

370
00:20:09,130 --> 00:20:11,825
Yeah, OK.

371
00:20:11,825 --> 00:20:12,950
Yeah, I think you're right.

372
00:20:12,950 --> 00:20:15,810
So k should be the number of groups,

373
00:20:15,810 --> 00:20:20,130
and g should be the number of heads per one

374
00:20:20,130 --> 00:20:21,230
of those groups.

375
00:20:21,230 --> 00:20:21,730
Yes.

376
00:20:21,730 --> 00:20:22,590
Yeah, OK.

377
00:20:22,590 --> 00:20:23,310
Thanks for that.

378
00:20:23,310 --> 00:20:24,270
I'll fix that later.

379
00:20:27,920 --> 00:20:30,180
OK.

380
00:20:30,180 --> 00:20:32,560
So let's talk a little bit more about the arithmetic

381
00:20:32,560 --> 00:20:34,320
intensity of inference.

382
00:20:34,320 --> 00:20:41,860
So here's how to think about what's happening in inference.

383
00:20:41,860 --> 00:20:44,280
Let's say you just do it naively.

384
00:20:44,280 --> 00:20:48,640
So here is a prompt never going to give you.

385
00:20:48,640 --> 00:20:50,720
I'm going to the transformer.

386
00:20:50,720 --> 00:20:55,360
And the transformer generates keys and values

387
00:20:55,360 --> 00:20:56,800
and activations.

388
00:20:56,800 --> 00:21:00,000
And at the end of the day, it generates logits

389
00:21:00,000 --> 00:21:02,720
over the output vocabulary.

390
00:21:02,720 --> 00:21:06,760
And you sample a token, and that token

391
00:21:06,760 --> 00:21:09,920
gets concatenated with the prompt,

392
00:21:09,920 --> 00:21:11,860
and then you just do this again.

393
00:21:11,860 --> 00:21:12,960
You generate never.

394
00:21:12,960 --> 00:21:16,720
You attach that to the prompt and so on and so forth.

395
00:21:16,720 --> 00:21:18,620
So this is the most naive thing.

396
00:21:18,620 --> 00:21:21,920
If you have a black box that takes a sequence

397
00:21:21,920 --> 00:21:24,110
and outputs a distribution over tokens, which

398
00:21:24,110 --> 00:21:25,650
is exactly what a transformer does,

399
00:21:25,650 --> 00:21:28,750
you can just apply this repeatedly.

400
00:21:28,750 --> 00:21:36,190
OK, so this works, but this is really bad because each time you

401
00:21:36,190 --> 00:21:40,150
generate one token, you actually take

402
00:21:40,150 --> 00:21:43,270
order T squared time where T is the number of tokens

403
00:21:43,270 --> 00:21:45,130
that you've generated so far.

404
00:21:45,130 --> 00:21:52,590
So generating T tokens is actually T cubed because the attention is

405
00:21:52,590 --> 00:22:00,910
already T squared, and you have to do that once for every token.

406
00:22:00,910 --> 00:22:05,830
So this is pretty bad, but the observation

407
00:22:05,830 --> 00:22:09,550
is that you don't actually have to do this.

408
00:22:09,550 --> 00:22:12,610
A lot of the work can actually be shared across prefixes.

409
00:22:12,610 --> 00:22:15,310
So for example, if you're generating network versus you're

410
00:22:15,310 --> 00:22:17,230
generating up, you're actually computing

411
00:22:17,230 --> 00:22:24,020
a lot of the same key values for never going to give.

412
00:22:24,020 --> 00:22:27,500
So these tokens, they shouldn't change, OK?

413
00:22:27,500 --> 00:22:30,040
And this is because it's a causal transformer.

414
00:22:30,040 --> 00:22:32,420
If it was bidirectional, then if you attach a token,

415
00:22:32,420 --> 00:22:34,060
then everything changes.

416
00:22:34,060 --> 00:22:36,340
But if it's causal, then the activations

417
00:22:36,340 --> 00:22:41,980
here don't change based on any tokens you append.

418
00:22:41,980 --> 00:22:45,740
So with that observation, the first obvious thing

419
00:22:45,740 --> 00:22:50,500
is to store a KV cache in HBM, so

420
00:22:50,500 --> 00:22:52,900
that, between successive token generations,

421
00:22:52,900 --> 00:22:55,300
you can just reuse the KV cache.

422
00:22:55,300 --> 00:23:02,100
So that means when you are trying to generate a token, you

423
00:23:02,100 --> 00:23:05,620
don't actually have to compute all the keys and activations

424
00:23:05,620 --> 00:23:10,460
of these previous tokens.

425
00:23:10,460 --> 00:23:12,680
So this is what it looks like with a KV cache.

426
00:23:12,680 --> 00:23:16,640
So there's going to be two stages: pre-fill.

427
00:23:16,640 --> 00:23:21,010
So you get the prompt, and you populate the KV cache, which

428
00:23:21,010 --> 00:23:25,930
is the set of key and value pairs that the transformer computes,

429
00:23:25,930 --> 00:23:27,750
and then you generate the logits--

430
00:23:27,750 --> 00:23:30,330
just the same computation as we did before.

431
00:23:30,330 --> 00:23:36,650
And now you have the KV cache here and then this distribution

432
00:23:36,650 --> 00:23:38,570
which you sample a token.

433
00:23:38,570 --> 00:23:41,490
And now you feed that through the transformer, which

434
00:23:41,490 --> 00:23:47,810
uses this cache and then produces

435
00:23:47,810 --> 00:23:50,070
both the distribution of the next tokens,

436
00:23:50,070 --> 00:23:54,090
but a new KV, which is basically activations

437
00:23:54,090 --> 00:23:57,370
corresponding to the token up.

438
00:23:57,370 --> 00:23:59,410
And then this gets fed in.

439
00:23:59,410 --> 00:24:02,210
So now you have the updated KV cache.

440
00:24:02,210 --> 00:24:05,890
You have this logits you sample, and then that

441
00:24:05,890 --> 00:24:07,390
feeds to the transformer.

442
00:24:07,390 --> 00:24:10,130
You output the distribution over next tokens

443
00:24:10,130 --> 00:24:15,250
as well as the new token which you add to the KV cache

444
00:24:15,250 --> 00:24:16,310
and so on and so forth.

445
00:24:19,960 --> 00:24:24,420
So the KV cache formula is for every sequence--

446
00:24:24,420 --> 00:24:25,620
so there's B of them.

447
00:24:25,620 --> 00:24:28,520
For every token, there's S of them.

448
00:24:28,520 --> 00:24:32,920
And for every layer and every head,

449
00:24:32,920 --> 00:24:34,820
you store H dimensional vector.

450
00:24:38,120 --> 00:24:41,480
And just to reiterate for inference, there's prefill.

451
00:24:41,480 --> 00:24:42,860
You're given a prompt.

452
00:24:42,860 --> 00:24:48,860
You encode the prompt into these vectors.

453
00:24:48,860 --> 00:24:51,000
And this is parallelizable just like in training

454
00:24:51,000 --> 00:24:52,500
because you see the entire prompt.

455
00:24:52,500 --> 00:24:55,240
You can compute the KV cache.

456
00:24:55,240 --> 00:24:59,560
And then in generation, you generate the new response tokens

457
00:24:59,560 --> 00:25:00,600
sequentially.

458
00:25:00,600 --> 00:25:02,240
But at least you don't have to pay

459
00:25:02,240 --> 00:25:05,160
for generating the KV cache of the tokens

460
00:25:05,160 --> 00:25:08,200
that you already looked at.

461
00:25:08,200 --> 00:25:11,300
So is everyone comfortable with the KV cache?

462
00:25:11,300 --> 00:25:12,860
Any questions about this?

463
00:25:22,020 --> 00:25:27,420
All right, so let's try to figure out the FLOPs and memory

464
00:25:27,420 --> 00:25:32,100
I/O for both MLP and attention layers.

465
00:25:32,100 --> 00:25:33,940
So remember S is the number of tokens

466
00:25:33,940 --> 00:25:37,340
we're kind of conditioning on, and T is the number of tokens

467
00:25:37,340 --> 00:25:39,260
we're generating.

468
00:25:39,260 --> 00:25:41,120
So we're going to do this abstractly.

469
00:25:41,120 --> 00:25:44,780
And then later, we'll specify to the prefill where T equals S,

470
00:25:44,780 --> 00:25:48,740
and generation T equals 1.

471
00:25:48,740 --> 00:25:51,140
So for the MLP layers-- and through all of this,

472
00:25:51,140 --> 00:25:52,880
I'm just going to look at the matmul

473
00:25:52,880 --> 00:25:55,140
because the matmuls are the thing that

474
00:25:55,140 --> 00:25:57,920
actually require a lot of work.

475
00:25:57,920 --> 00:26:02,180
Everything else is not that many FLOPs

476
00:26:02,180 --> 00:26:06,760
and can be potentially fused into the matmul too.

477
00:26:06,760 --> 00:26:07,260
OK.

478
00:26:07,260 --> 00:26:09,440
So let's do this calculation.

479
00:26:09,440 --> 00:26:12,000
So it's the same as the matrix calculations.

480
00:26:12,000 --> 00:26:15,890
I'm not going to maybe belabor the step through every single detail

481
00:26:15,890 --> 00:26:19,610
here, but just the form is you read X

482
00:26:19,610 --> 00:26:24,130
from high bandwidth memory.

483
00:26:24,130 --> 00:26:27,570
You read all the parameters.

484
00:26:27,570 --> 00:26:33,130
You compute the upper projection,

485
00:26:33,130 --> 00:26:35,610
and then you write it to HBM.

486
00:26:35,610 --> 00:26:42,850
You compute the gate, and you write HBM,

487
00:26:42,850 --> 00:26:51,050
and then you compute basically the down projection of that,

488
00:26:51,050 --> 00:26:53,970
and you write it to HBM.

489
00:26:53,970 --> 00:26:59,690
So the number of flops depends on B and T

490
00:26:59,690 --> 00:27:02,970
and D and F. So batch size, sequence length,

491
00:27:02,970 --> 00:27:07,170
model dimension, and the feedforward dimension,

492
00:27:07,170 --> 00:27:08,450
MLP dimension.

493
00:27:08,450 --> 00:27:15,040
And the bytes transferred is this expression.

494
00:27:15,040 --> 00:27:17,640
So now you can compute the arithmetic intensity

495
00:27:17,640 --> 00:27:22,200
which, again, is FLOPs divided by bytes transferred.

496
00:27:22,200 --> 00:27:24,400
So this is some expression.

497
00:27:24,400 --> 00:27:27,440
We're going to assume, that just like before, B times T

498
00:27:27,440 --> 00:27:33,720
is much smaller than D and F. And with that, then we see

499
00:27:33,720 --> 00:27:38,840
that intensity is B times T.

500
00:27:38,840 --> 00:27:41,900
So this is analogous to just the MAmmoTH case.

501
00:27:41,900 --> 00:27:44,380
There's more matrices and there's more dimensions,

502
00:27:44,380 --> 00:27:46,225
but fundamentally it's the same thing.

503
00:27:46,225 --> 00:27:47,100
And that makes sense.

504
00:27:47,100 --> 00:27:50,620
An MLP is basically a big MAmmoTH.

505
00:27:50,620 --> 00:27:55,760
And it also makes sense because the batch dimension

506
00:27:55,760 --> 00:27:59,660
and the sequence length dimension are--

507
00:27:59,660 --> 00:28:01,840
everything is independent.

508
00:28:01,840 --> 00:28:04,860
And for the MLP, they don't interact.

509
00:28:04,860 --> 00:28:06,600
Attention is different story.

510
00:28:06,600 --> 00:28:12,350
And so B times T. So as long as you have--

511
00:28:12,350 --> 00:28:15,270
in the pre-fill, if you make B times T

512
00:28:15,270 --> 00:28:19,030
large enough-- large batches, long sequences-- you'll be fine.

513
00:28:19,030 --> 00:28:20,690
Now, let's look at generation.

514
00:28:20,690 --> 00:28:24,430
So remember, generation, there's two problems here.

515
00:28:24,430 --> 00:28:28,930
One is that generation T equals 1.

516
00:28:28,930 --> 00:28:33,030
You're only generating one token at a time.

517
00:28:33,030 --> 00:28:35,710
And so that means your arithmetic intensity

518
00:28:35,710 --> 00:28:40,230
is going to be B, but B in generation

519
00:28:40,230 --> 00:28:43,270
is the number of concurrent requests.

520
00:28:43,270 --> 00:28:46,730
And so if you're in a batch setting, you can control that.

521
00:28:46,730 --> 00:28:48,967
But if you're, let's say, serving a chatbot,

522
00:28:48,967 --> 00:28:50,550
it's the number of concurrent requests

523
00:28:50,550 --> 00:28:55,150
is essentially how many users, concurrent users there are?

524
00:28:55,150 --> 00:28:56,170
Which can be high.

525
00:28:56,170 --> 00:28:56,850
It can be low.

526
00:28:56,850 --> 00:28:58,330
So it's a bit unpredictable.

527
00:28:58,330 --> 00:28:59,547
It can be changing over time.

528
00:28:59,547 --> 00:29:01,630
So that's something we're going to address when we

529
00:29:01,630 --> 00:29:06,630
talk about continuous batching.

530
00:29:06,630 --> 00:29:10,980
But overall, this is not bad because as long

531
00:29:10,980 --> 00:29:14,388
as you have large batches, the sequence length

532
00:29:14,388 --> 00:29:15,680
isn't going to really help you.

533
00:29:15,680 --> 00:29:18,860
But if you have large batches, you should be good.

534
00:29:18,860 --> 00:29:22,460
So now let's look at the attention layer.

535
00:29:22,460 --> 00:29:25,760
So again, S is the previous tokens already generated.

536
00:29:25,760 --> 00:29:29,900
T is the number of tokens you want to generate logits for.

537
00:29:29,900 --> 00:29:40,500
So in attention, you read the QKV matrices from HBM.

538
00:29:40,500 --> 00:29:43,580
You compute the attention.

539
00:29:43,580 --> 00:29:47,380
You compute the softmax which doesn't really matter,

540
00:29:47,380 --> 00:29:51,820
and compute the value matrix, and then you

541
00:29:51,820 --> 00:29:54,460
write the result to HBM.

542
00:29:54,460 --> 00:29:59,020
So the FLOPs is B times S times T times D,

543
00:29:59,020 --> 00:30:04,420
and the bytes transferred is this expression.

544
00:30:04,420 --> 00:30:07,830
Everything is a matmul, right?

545
00:30:07,830 --> 00:30:13,650
So this should be always one degree higher of a polynomial

546
00:30:13,650 --> 00:30:17,450
than the bytes transferred because it's a matmul.

547
00:30:17,450 --> 00:30:21,850
The only question is, what does that factor look like?

548
00:30:21,850 --> 00:30:29,570
So that factor for attention is S times T over S plus T.

549
00:30:29,570 --> 00:30:33,170
So let's look at the prefill.

550
00:30:33,170 --> 00:30:37,210
So prefill, what this is saying is

551
00:30:37,210 --> 00:30:41,970
that the prefill intensity is S over 2 when T equals

552
00:30:41,970 --> 00:30:44,270
S. So this is good, right?

553
00:30:44,270 --> 00:30:49,210
Because as long as you have long sequence lengths for attention,

554
00:30:49,210 --> 00:30:54,890
you're going to maintain high arithmetic intensity.

555
00:30:54,890 --> 00:30:58,190
Notice that the batching dimension doesn't happen here.

556
00:30:58,190 --> 00:31:02,330
I'll explain a bit why later.

557
00:31:02,330 --> 00:31:07,360
But for generation, this is bad news.

558
00:31:07,360 --> 00:31:12,200
So the generation intensity is S over S plus 1,

559
00:31:12,200 --> 00:31:16,960
and that's less than 1, or even let's just call it 1.

560
00:31:16,960 --> 00:31:19,340
And arithmetic intensity, remember 1 is bad.

561
00:31:19,340 --> 00:31:22,120
We want it to be something like 295 for H100

562
00:31:22,120 --> 00:31:24,520
to be able to saturate the compute.

563
00:31:24,520 --> 00:31:29,860
And so this is really the bottleneck.

564
00:31:29,860 --> 00:31:35,680
So we did all this analysis for MLP attention,

565
00:31:35,680 --> 00:31:39,640
prefilled generation, and we've found this to be a bottleneck.

566
00:31:39,640 --> 00:31:46,400
So let's try to contemplate why this is a bottleneck.

567
00:31:46,400 --> 00:31:50,800
So unlike MLPs-- so MLP is for generation is actually OK

568
00:31:50,800 --> 00:31:52,760
as long as your sequence is--

569
00:31:52,760 --> 00:31:59,080
sorry, your batch is large enough.

570
00:31:59,080 --> 00:32:07,790
So the problem with this MLP attention is that--

571
00:32:07,790 --> 00:32:09,430
let's look at MLPs.

572
00:32:09,430 --> 00:32:14,670
Every sequence hits the same MLP weights.

573
00:32:14,670 --> 00:32:19,830
So these don't depend on B. Whereas, in attention layer,

574
00:32:19,830 --> 00:32:23,390
each sequence has its own KV cache.

575
00:32:23,390 --> 00:32:27,830
So these all depend on MLP.

576
00:32:27,830 --> 00:32:36,230
And so you can think about this as what's going on here

577
00:32:36,230 --> 00:32:43,070
is that in the MLP case, having B being big actually

578
00:32:43,070 --> 00:32:48,510
is helpful because you get to load these MLP weights once.

579
00:32:48,510 --> 00:32:52,230
And you can use it for all your sequences.

580
00:32:52,230 --> 00:32:54,830
That's how you get high arithmetic intensity

581
00:32:54,830 --> 00:32:58,510
because you use it--simplifying a bit, you load it once.

582
00:32:58,510 --> 00:33:02,790
You do all your batch processing, and then that is good.

583
00:33:02,790 --> 00:33:09,010
Whereas, in attention, all these depend on B. So increasing B

584
00:33:09,010 --> 00:33:09,950
doesn't help.

585
00:33:09,950 --> 00:33:12,810
It's like for every sequence, you're

586
00:33:12,810 --> 00:33:15,490
basically doing a matmul.

587
00:33:15,490 --> 00:33:17,430
So they're all independent.

588
00:33:17,430 --> 00:33:21,890
So doing more matmuls isn't helpful.

589
00:33:21,890 --> 00:33:24,750
Just if you remember in the very beginning,

590
00:33:24,750 --> 00:33:27,250
I showed you this example.

591
00:33:27,250 --> 00:33:31,270
This also has pretty bad arithmetic intensity.

592
00:33:31,270 --> 00:33:33,490
It's not a matmul because we're essentially

593
00:33:33,490 --> 00:33:37,130
batching by a coordinate.

594
00:33:37,130 --> 00:33:39,210
And this is essentially basically the same

595
00:33:39,210 --> 00:33:43,370
as doing a dot product, which has horrible arithmetic

596
00:33:43,370 --> 00:33:44,210
intensity.

597
00:33:44,210 --> 00:33:46,430
And remember, this is in the attention.

598
00:33:46,430 --> 00:33:52,210
This blue B is the cause of why the attention is

599
00:33:52,210 --> 00:33:54,410
arithmetic intensity doesn't scale with B,

600
00:33:54,410 --> 00:33:58,290
and why that is a bottleneck.

601
00:33:58,290 --> 00:34:00,170
So just to summarize here.

602
00:34:00,170 --> 00:34:02,260
So prefill is compute-bound.

603
00:34:02,260 --> 00:34:04,640
Generation is memory-bound.

604
00:34:04,640 --> 00:34:10,560
So if you look at the MLP intensity for prefill,

605
00:34:10,560 --> 00:34:12,480
it's B times S, great.

606
00:34:12,480 --> 00:34:18,040
Prefill for attention, intensity S over 2--

607
00:34:18,040 --> 00:34:20,400
not as good, but workable.

608
00:34:20,400 --> 00:34:24,040
Generation MLP intensity, also workable,

609
00:34:24,040 --> 00:34:26,679
requires long concurrent requests.

610
00:34:26,679 --> 00:34:29,920
But it is really the generation attention

611
00:34:29,920 --> 00:34:36,880
intensity which is a fundamental bottleneck, and that's it.

612
00:34:36,880 --> 00:34:38,659
If you're sticking with a transformer,

613
00:34:38,659 --> 00:34:43,639
you can't really improve this.

614
00:34:43,639 --> 00:34:44,880
OK.

615
00:34:44,880 --> 00:34:46,860
Pause there for questions.

616
00:34:57,560 --> 00:35:00,550
So now whenever you hear people say, oh, inference

617
00:35:00,550 --> 00:35:03,510
is memory bound, you know why.

618
00:35:07,070 --> 00:35:07,750
OK.

619
00:35:07,750 --> 00:35:13,270
So let's now use these intuitions and calculations

620
00:35:13,270 --> 00:35:17,550
to think about our inference metrics

621
00:35:17,550 --> 00:35:21,230
throughput and latency and also TFRT.

622
00:35:21,230 --> 00:35:21,730
All right.

623
00:35:21,730 --> 00:35:24,410
So inference is memory bound.

624
00:35:27,310 --> 00:35:30,110
Now the main-- I mean, in some ways

625
00:35:30,110 --> 00:35:32,310
this simplifies a lot of things because when

626
00:35:32,310 --> 00:35:34,150
we think about how long things take,

627
00:35:34,150 --> 00:35:38,590
you just look at how much memory needs to be transferred, OK?

628
00:35:38,590 --> 00:35:44,230
Because assuming you overlap communication and computation,

629
00:35:44,230 --> 00:35:47,630
the bottleneck is going to be just the amount of memory

630
00:35:47,630 --> 00:35:49,470
that you have to deal with.

631
00:35:49,470 --> 00:35:52,170
So in some ways, it's nice because it's simpler.

632
00:35:52,170 --> 00:35:54,710
But in other ways, it's frustrating

633
00:35:54,710 --> 00:35:58,080
that your accelerators are sitting there not doing anything.

634
00:36:00,660 --> 00:36:04,240
So let's walk through an example.

635
00:36:04,240 --> 00:36:09,460
So for Llama 2.13B on an H100.

636
00:36:09,460 --> 00:36:12,500
So what is the latency and the throughput?

637
00:36:12,500 --> 00:36:16,960
So remember Llama 2.13B has a particular shape.

638
00:36:16,960 --> 00:36:21,060
So this is a sequence length, the model dimension,

639
00:36:21,060 --> 00:36:25,560
feedforward dimension, number of query, number key value heads.

640
00:36:25,560 --> 00:36:26,760
There's no GQA here.

641
00:36:26,760 --> 00:36:29,700
So N equals k, head dimension,

642
00:36:29,700 --> 00:36:34,500
number of layers, vocab size, and the memory bandwidth

643
00:36:34,500 --> 00:36:41,820
for H100.

644
00:36:41,820 --> 00:36:46,740
So now, using this config, let's compute

645
00:36:46,740 --> 00:36:50,960
the transformer performance statistics, OK?

646
00:36:50,960 --> 00:36:55,450
So these are our inputs here.

647
00:36:55,450 --> 00:36:58,930
And just the statistics I'm going to compute

648
00:36:58,930 --> 00:37:05,570
are going to be number of parameters, the memory usage,

649
00:37:05,570 --> 00:37:07,810
latency, and throughput.

650
00:37:07,810 --> 00:37:16,290
So first of all, what takes memory?

651
00:37:16,290 --> 00:37:18,610
So the parameters take memory.

652
00:37:18,610 --> 00:37:23,170
So we compute the number of parameters.

653
00:37:23,170 --> 00:37:29,930
And so you look at the embeddings, the MLP layers,

654
00:37:29,930 --> 00:37:34,010
the projections of the KQV.

655
00:37:34,010 --> 00:37:39,450
At the end of the day, you get some number of parameters.

656
00:37:39,450 --> 00:37:44,370
Assuming that we're doing a bf16, which is always

657
00:37:44,370 --> 00:37:46,490
the case for inference, then parameters

658
00:37:46,490 --> 00:37:49,330
take up this many bytes.

659
00:37:49,330 --> 00:37:53,280
Also in memory is the KV cache.

660
00:37:53,280 --> 00:37:58,800
And the KV cache is the number of tokens in your sequence

661
00:37:58,800 --> 00:38:07,200
times the number of heads and the KV heads times the head

662
00:38:07,200 --> 00:38:09,200
dimension times the number of layers.

663
00:38:09,200 --> 00:38:12,780
You have one for the key and one for the value,

664
00:38:12,780 --> 00:38:16,580
and then you have multiple two for bf16.

665
00:38:16,580 --> 00:38:18,960
So that's the size of the KV cache.

666
00:38:18,960 --> 00:38:21,720
And the total memory usage is--

667
00:38:21,720 --> 00:38:24,740
this is for each sequence you have B sequences.

668
00:38:24,740 --> 00:38:28,120
So that's B times that plus the parameter size.

669
00:38:28,120 --> 00:38:32,000
And that's the amount of memory you need.

670
00:38:32,000 --> 00:38:34,300
And now, what's the latency?

671
00:38:34,300 --> 00:38:41,520
The latency is determined by the memory I/O, OK?

672
00:38:41,520 --> 00:38:46,160
Because inference is memory-bound, most assuming you

673
00:38:46,160 --> 00:38:49,720
overlap communication and compute.

674
00:38:49,720 --> 00:38:56,510
All the memory, it's going to be on shuffling parameters back

675
00:38:56,510 --> 00:39:01,670
and forth between HBM and SRAM.

676
00:39:01,670 --> 00:39:06,870
So that's how long it takes to move memory around.

677
00:39:06,870 --> 00:39:09,690
Throughput is the inverse of latency,

678
00:39:09,690 --> 00:39:12,210
but we're generating B tokens in parallel.

679
00:39:12,210 --> 00:39:16,170
So this is tokens per second.

680
00:39:16,170 --> 00:39:17,290
This is seconds per token.

681
00:39:17,290 --> 00:39:19,830
This is tokens per second, as well as

682
00:39:19,830 --> 00:39:27,290
having an additional B because you're processing a batch of B.

683
00:39:27,290 --> 00:39:31,870
So now, let's compute for this config what

684
00:39:31,870 --> 00:39:33,590
are these actual values?

685
00:39:33,590 --> 00:39:39,390
So num parameters is 13 billion.

686
00:39:39,390 --> 00:39:42,510
So a good sanity check-- that is advertised as a 13 billion

687
00:39:42,510 --> 00:39:44,390
parameter model.

688
00:39:44,390 --> 00:39:53,090
Memory is this term, which is about 838 million times B.

689
00:39:53,090 --> 00:39:55,650
So it's basically a linear function

690
00:39:55,650 --> 00:39:58,510
of B plus some other term.

691
00:39:58,510 --> 00:40:00,570
So this is a KV cache which grows

692
00:40:00,570 --> 00:40:02,930
as B. This is the parameters, which

693
00:40:02,930 --> 00:40:06,370
is double the number of params.

694
00:40:06,370 --> 00:40:10,630
Now, latency is just this scale by the memory bandwidth,

695
00:40:10,630 --> 00:40:15,050
so it has the same form as the memory.

696
00:40:15,050 --> 00:40:17,430
And throughput is B over that.

697
00:40:20,850 --> 00:40:24,610
So notice that latency, as you increase B,

698
00:40:24,610 --> 00:40:28,630
grows because the KV cache grows.

699
00:40:28,630 --> 00:40:30,090
And in order to process stuff, you

700
00:40:30,090 --> 00:40:33,450
have to copy the KV cache back and forth.

701
00:40:33,450 --> 00:40:35,930
And now, throughput is more interesting

702
00:40:35,930 --> 00:40:39,250
because, as you increase B, throughput

703
00:40:39,250 --> 00:40:45,250
does improve but up to a limit.

704
00:40:45,250 --> 00:40:47,530
Throughput improves because now you're

705
00:40:47,530 --> 00:40:50,760
amortizing the cost over a larger batch.

706
00:40:50,760 --> 00:40:54,280
But also, the speed at which you're processing this obviously

707
00:40:54,280 --> 00:40:58,480
is also increasing over time.

708
00:40:58,480 --> 00:41:00,720
So this asymptotes.

709
00:41:00,720 --> 00:41:03,980
It's not going to--throughput can't possibly go to infinity.

710
00:41:06,600 --> 00:41:10,820
Any questions about this so far?

711
00:41:14,360 --> 00:41:16,720
So basically, you just have to remember

712
00:41:16,720 --> 00:41:21,320
latency is a linear function in B. This is where the constant is

713
00:41:21,320 --> 00:41:24,160
the size of KV cache, and then this the number of parameters.

714
00:41:24,160 --> 00:41:30,720
And then throughput is some proportional to B over B

715
00:41:30,720 --> 00:41:31,820
plus something.

716
00:41:35,040 --> 00:41:40,140
So now, let's instantiate this for a bunch of situations, OK?

717
00:41:40,140 --> 00:41:46,280
So if you have batch size 1, so this is what you would get.

718
00:41:46,280 --> 00:41:55,470
You get a latency of 0.008 seconds per token.

719
00:41:55,470 --> 00:41:59,110
And the throughput is 124 tokens per second.

720
00:41:59,110 --> 00:42:02,070
So what happens if you increase the batch size,

721
00:42:02,070 --> 00:42:07,470
you'll see that the latency goes up,

722
00:42:07,470 --> 00:42:09,330
but the throughput also goes up.

723
00:42:09,330 --> 00:42:13,070
So latency gets worse, but the throughput improves.

724
00:42:13,070 --> 00:42:16,710
So this is an interesting thing because we think about, oh, we

725
00:42:16,710 --> 00:42:18,370
just want to make it go fast.

726
00:42:18,370 --> 00:42:19,910
But fast actually has two meanings

727
00:42:19,910 --> 00:42:22,870
here, which actually, depending on which one you care about,

728
00:42:22,870 --> 00:42:24,610
is completely opposite.

729
00:42:24,610 --> 00:42:27,110
If you want to tune your batch size,

730
00:42:27,110 --> 00:42:30,670
that's going to really determine if you

731
00:42:30,670 --> 00:42:33,630
want latency or throughput.

732
00:42:33,630 --> 00:42:36,710
And what happens if you increase-- let's say, increase--

733
00:42:36,710 --> 00:42:38,790
we really want high throughput because we're

734
00:42:38,790 --> 00:42:40,410
processing lots of documents.

735
00:42:40,410 --> 00:42:42,970
Let's increase the batch size even more, OK?

736
00:42:42,970 --> 00:42:45,620
So the latency gets even worse.

737
00:42:45,620 --> 00:42:47,380
I mean, yeah, it gets worse.

738
00:42:47,380 --> 00:42:49,580
Throughput gets even better.

739
00:42:49,580 --> 00:42:53,900
But the main problem here is that your memory, you run out

740
00:42:53,900 --> 00:42:59,660
of memory because the memory, for storing this KV cache,

741
00:42:59,660 --> 00:43:03,820
is going to exceed your H100 memory.

742
00:43:03,820 --> 00:43:06,440
And if you have B200s, you can increase the batch size more,

743
00:43:06,440 --> 00:43:09,180
but eventually you hit some limit.

744
00:43:09,180 --> 00:43:11,780
So there's limitations to how much

745
00:43:11,780 --> 00:43:13,320
you can increase your throughput.

746
00:43:13,320 --> 00:43:15,980
You'll never get to the asymptote

747
00:43:15,980 --> 00:43:20,700
because you'll hit the memory.

748
00:43:20,700 --> 00:43:24,940
And also, your throughput is getting--

749
00:43:24,940 --> 00:43:27,400
your gains are kind of diminishing as well.

750
00:43:30,780 --> 00:43:34,780
So increasing batch size worsens the latency

751
00:43:34,780 --> 00:43:40,260
because now you have a larger KV cache to read and write.

752
00:43:40,260 --> 00:43:41,980
And remember, it's batched.

753
00:43:41,980 --> 00:43:43,750
So if you're an individual query,

754
00:43:43,750 --> 00:43:45,490
you have to wait for everyone to finish.

755
00:43:45,490 --> 00:43:48,650
So you have to basically-- you're waiting for a bus,

756
00:43:48,650 --> 00:43:51,050
and the latency is pretty high.

757
00:43:51,050 --> 00:43:54,170
You wait and then you go.

758
00:43:54,170 --> 00:43:56,210
Whereas, the throughput of a bus is pretty good

759
00:43:56,210 --> 00:43:59,930
because you can move everyone at once.

760
00:43:59,930 --> 00:44:04,190
So the throughput improves as batch size increases

761
00:44:04,190 --> 00:44:08,190
because, remember, the parameters are shared,

762
00:44:08,190 --> 00:44:12,070
and you load that once into memory.

763
00:44:12,070 --> 00:44:14,630
And you can process a lot of sequences.

764
00:44:17,330 --> 00:44:20,290
So trade-off between latency and throughput,

765
00:44:20,290 --> 00:44:23,210
just to make sure everyone's aligned—smaller batch sizes

766
00:44:23,210 --> 00:44:25,430
use better latency but worse throughput.

767
00:44:25,430 --> 00:44:29,770
Large batch sizes yield better throughput but worse latency.

768
00:44:29,770 --> 00:44:30,970
OK.

769
00:44:30,970 --> 00:44:33,630
I'm not going to really talk about parallelism too much.

770
00:44:33,630 --> 00:44:35,510
There's also another dimension of inference,

771
00:44:35,510 --> 00:44:39,530
which is you can shard your model

772
00:44:39,530 --> 00:44:43,000
across multiple devices.

773
00:44:43,000 --> 00:44:45,440
You can look at the scaling book chapter on inference

774
00:44:45,440 --> 00:44:47,600
if you want to know more.

775
00:44:47,600 --> 00:44:50,680
Just as a very trivial example, if you

776
00:44:50,680 --> 00:44:53,760
launch M copies of the model, the latency is the same,

777
00:44:53,760 --> 00:44:58,760
and the throughput increases by M.

778
00:44:58,760 --> 00:45:01,000
And then the other metric we didn't talk about

779
00:45:01,000 --> 00:45:02,880
is time to first token.

780
00:45:02,880 --> 00:45:04,760
And this is essentially the time it

781
00:45:04,760 --> 00:45:07,840
takes to do prefill because after you finish prefill,

782
00:45:07,840 --> 00:45:11,840
then you can basically start generating.

783
00:45:11,840 --> 00:45:20,680
So if you want to fast—

784
00:45:20,680 --> 00:45:26,360
if you want faster TTFT, you should use smaller batch sizes,

785
00:45:26,360 --> 00:45:30,560
and you want larger batch sizes to improve throughput.

786
00:45:30,560 --> 00:45:34,820
So hopefully, that is clear.

787
00:45:34,820 --> 00:45:37,320
Any questions about throughput and latency

788
00:45:37,320 --> 00:45:39,420
and how they interact with each other?

789
00:45:47,830 --> 00:45:48,630
OK.

790
00:45:48,630 --> 00:45:51,510
So now that we have a conceptual framework for thinking

791
00:45:51,510 --> 00:45:57,830
about how efficient inference is in terms of arithmetic intensity

792
00:45:57,830 --> 00:46:03,810
and throughput and latency, now let's try to make it faster.

793
00:46:03,810 --> 00:46:05,210
How do we make inference faster?

794
00:46:05,210 --> 00:46:07,830
There's a bunch of different techniques which

795
00:46:07,830 --> 00:46:11,150
are quite varied, ranging from changing the model architecture

796
00:46:11,150 --> 00:46:13,690
to doing systems optimization and everything in between.

797
00:46:13,690 --> 00:46:20,350
So inference in some sense is a fairly rich cross-cutting topic.

798
00:46:20,350 --> 00:46:27,550
So the most maybe now in hindsight obvious thing

799
00:46:27,550 --> 00:46:31,430
you can think about is hopefully beat it into your head

800
00:46:31,430 --> 00:46:35,630
that memory is the bottleneck for inference,

801
00:46:35,630 --> 00:46:39,050
and the KV cache takes up a lot of memory.

802
00:46:39,050 --> 00:46:42,410
And it could even be larger than the number of parameters

803
00:46:42,410 --> 00:46:44,330
if for a large enough batch size.

804
00:46:44,330 --> 00:46:48,450
So let's just try to reduce the size of the KV cache.

805
00:46:48,450 --> 00:46:50,810
Now, you have to be careful about how you do this

806
00:46:50,810 --> 00:46:52,810
because you want to make sure you don't lose too

807
00:46:52,810 --> 00:46:56,050
much accuracy in the process.

808
00:46:56,050 --> 00:47:00,750
So here is one thing you can do, which we already talked about,

809
00:47:00,750 --> 00:47:03,450
which is grouped query attention.

810
00:47:03,450 --> 00:47:09,490
And just as a reminder here, so multi-headed attention--

811
00:47:09,490 --> 00:47:11,250
basically for every token, you have

812
00:47:11,250 --> 00:47:13,850
a key and a value and a query.

813
00:47:13,850 --> 00:47:16,930
And if you do grouped query attention,

814
00:47:16,930 --> 00:47:20,610
you basically compute the same number of queries,

815
00:47:20,610 --> 00:47:24,570
but you have only a smaller number of groups,

816
00:47:24,570 --> 00:47:28,930
and you compute key and value for each group.

817
00:47:28,930 --> 00:47:36,680
So K is the number of groups here.

818
00:47:36,680 --> 00:47:41,560
And so in the multi-headed attention, K is N, no reduction.

819
00:47:41,560 --> 00:47:44,200
There's something called multi query attention, which no one

820
00:47:44,200 --> 00:47:46,120
uses because it's really bad.

821
00:47:46,120 --> 00:47:47,440
K equals 1.

822
00:47:47,440 --> 00:47:50,280
And somewhere in between is hopefully

823
00:47:50,280 --> 00:47:54,920
where we'll find a balance between accuracy and speed.

824
00:47:54,920 --> 00:47:59,720
So this is the paper that introduces GQA from 2023.

825
00:47:59,720 --> 00:48:05,600
And they show that if you look at time per sample, which

826
00:48:05,600 --> 00:48:09,920
is related to both latency and throughput,

827
00:48:09,920 --> 00:48:15,480
you see that the MHA-- so Multi-Head Attention.

828
00:48:15,480 --> 00:48:20,080
Full attention has this high time.

829
00:48:20,080 --> 00:48:24,880
Whereas, if you start K equals 1, it's much faster.

830
00:48:24,880 --> 00:48:29,118
And then you can actually keep on increasing the K to K

831
00:48:29,118 --> 00:48:30,660
equals 8, and it's still pretty good.

832
00:48:30,660 --> 00:48:36,910
And eventually, your time goes up quite a bit.

833
00:48:39,830 --> 00:48:43,950
So why does GQA improve latency and throughput?

834
00:48:43,950 --> 00:48:53,190
Well, it reduces the KV cache by a factor of N over K. And just

835
00:48:53,190 --> 00:48:58,190
as a friendly reminder, reducing memory usage leads to speed up

836
00:48:58,190 --> 00:49:00,270
because we're memory-bound.

837
00:49:00,270 --> 00:49:06,767
So let's revisit our friendly Llama model here.

838
00:49:06,767 --> 00:49:08,350
Remember in the initial configuration,

839
00:49:08,350 --> 00:49:11,230
we're just using K equals N. So there's

840
00:49:11,230 --> 00:49:15,110
没有多头——是multi-head attention，

841
00:49:15,110 --> 00:49:19,110
没有减少键和值的数量。

842
00:49:19,110 --> 00:49:25,030
所以对于这个，记住使用batch size为64，

843
00:49:25,030 --> 00:49:27,510
我们得到这个吞吐量和延迟。

844
00:49:27,510 --> 00:49:32,370
现在如果你用GQA，我们要放入，比如说，

845
00:49:32,370 --> 00:49:36,260
一种稀疏度为1/5。

846
00:49:36,260 --> 00:49:44,580
这大幅减少了内存，

847
00:49:44,580 --> 00:49:47,060
进而改善了延迟

848
00:49:47,060 --> 00:49:49,740
并且也提高了吞吐量。

849
00:49:49,740 --> 00:49:52,780
所以延迟和吞吐量并不总是相互矛盾的。

850
00:49:52,780 --> 00:49:56,540
如果你减少内存量，它就会同时改善两者。

851
00:49:56,540 --> 00:49:59,420
主要是batch维度允许的。

852
00:49:59,420 --> 00:50:01,800
那是紧张的焦点。

853
00:50:01,800 --> 00:50:02,300
好的。

854
00:50:02,300 --> 00:50:06,220
所以这很好。

855
00:50:06,220 --> 00:50:13,540
让我们实际上把batch size增加到更大

856
00:50:13,540 --> 00:50:15,860
看看会发生什么。

857
00:50:15,860 --> 00:50:20,780
所以之前，如果我们batch size为256，

858
00:50:20,780 --> 00:50:22,240
我们就内存不足了。

859
00:50:22,240 --> 00:50:24,340
但现在，它能装进内存了。

860
00:50:24,340 --> 00:50:32,690
我们看到，延迟受到了一些影响，因为我们

861
00:50:32,690 --> 00:50:36,570
增加了batch size，但吞吐量

862
00:50:36,570 --> 00:50:40,770
成比例上升。

863
00:50:40,770 --> 00:50:45,310
所以有时你要联合调整这些参数。

864
00:50:45,310 --> 00:50:47,090
你可以减少KV cache，但那

865
00:50:47,090 --> 00:50:48,850
允许你增加batch size

866
00:50:48,850 --> 00:50:52,810
并允许做出其他权衡。

867
00:50:52,810 --> 00:50:55,010
所以你必须做的最后一件事是，每当

868
00:50:55,010 --> 00:50:58,130
你做一些有损的改变时，你要

869
00:50:58,130 --> 00:50:59,970
确保准确率不会下降。

870
00:50:59,970 --> 00:51:08,470
这篇论文表明，对于GQA，时间是更好的。

871
00:51:08,470 --> 00:51:14,850
但在一系列评估中，基本上它运行良好。

872
00:51:14,850 --> 00:51:16,538
所以现在有了这些准确率值，

873
00:51:16,538 --> 00:51:18,830
我认为你总是要持保留态度

874
00:51:18,830 --> 00:51:20,790
因为这是针对特定模型的。

875
00:51:20,790 --> 00:51:24,390
后来，DeepSeek论文应该表明它实际上确实会有损害。

876
00:51:24,390 --> 00:51:28,530
所以我想，把所有

877
00:51:28,530 --> 00:51:32,760
不只是数学的东西都持保留态度吧。

878
00:51:32,760 --> 00:51:33,260
好的。

879
00:51:33,260 --> 00:51:36,320
所以说到DeepSeek，这里有另一个想法

880
00:51:36,320 --> 00:51:39,880
to reduce the KV cache.

881
00:51:39,880 --> 00:51:43,720
So the theme is reduce the KV cache and latency and throughput

882
00:51:43,720 --> 00:51:45,760
improve.

883
00:51:45,760 --> 00:51:49,280
So this is multi-headed attention,

884
00:51:49,280 --> 00:51:54,320
same number of queries and keys and values for every token.

885
00:51:54,320 --> 00:51:58,600
And GQA, remember, we have reduced the number of keys

886
00:51:58,600 --> 00:52:01,480
and values for every token.

887
00:52:01,480 --> 00:52:02,620
Sorry, not for every token.

888
00:52:02,620 --> 00:52:07,280
We basically have reduced the number of values and keys.

889
00:52:07,280 --> 00:52:11,440
And now, the multi-latent attention for DeepSeek

890
00:52:11,440 --> 00:52:15,960
says we're actually going to leave the number of keys

891
00:52:15,960 --> 00:52:21,148
and values the same one for every token essentially.

892
00:52:21,148 --> 00:52:24,480
But I'm going to parameterize--I'm going to compress these.

893
00:52:24,480 --> 00:52:27,990
So normally, you have some--

894
00:52:27,990 --> 00:52:29,690
how do you compute your keys and values?

895
00:52:29,690 --> 00:52:33,030
You have your activations, and you multiply them

896
00:52:33,030 --> 00:52:35,870
by some matrix to get K and some other matrix to get V.

897
00:52:35,870 --> 00:52:40,230
And these are generally N times H dimensions, like your model

898
00:52:40,230 --> 00:52:43,310
dim, which is big.

899
00:52:43,310 --> 00:52:46,910
So MLA says I'm going to actually project

900
00:52:46,910 --> 00:52:50,510
these activations down into C dimensions.

901
00:52:50,510 --> 00:52:55,050
So DeepSeek-V2 reduced it from 16,000 to 512,

902
00:52:55,050 --> 00:52:57,630
so this is quite aggressive compression here.

903
00:52:57,630 --> 00:53:00,830
And then I'm going to compute the k and the V

904
00:53:00,830 --> 00:53:04,510
from this compressed representation.

905
00:53:04,510 --> 00:53:07,990
So now, I can just store C. This is much smaller.

906
00:53:07,990 --> 00:53:09,790
And then when I need my keys and values,

907
00:53:09,790 --> 00:53:11,850
I can just materialize them.

908
00:53:14,390 --> 00:53:16,070
So there is one wrinkle here, which

909
00:53:16,070 --> 00:53:19,070
is that MLA is not compatible with rope,

910
00:53:19,070 --> 00:53:22,110
which operates directly on the keys and values.

911
00:53:22,110 --> 00:53:25,540
So what they do is add additional dimensions

912
00:53:25,540 --> 00:53:30,940
for handling the rope.

913
00:53:30,940 --> 00:53:36,100
But more or less, it's still a pretty big reduction.

914
00:53:36,100 --> 00:53:39,180
So the latency and throughput improvements

915
00:53:39,180 --> 00:53:42,200
follow by just simple math.

916
00:53:42,200 --> 00:53:47,940
The smaller the KV cache, the faster you go.

917
00:53:47,940 --> 00:53:52,420
It's almost kind of linear scaling up until at some point.

918
00:53:52,420 --> 00:53:54,020
And then, remember, you need to check

919
00:53:54,020 --> 00:53:56,900
whether your model is accurate.

920
00:53:56,900 --> 00:54:00,460
So first of all, this is the result

921
00:54:00,460 --> 00:54:07,060
that kind of contradicts or is in tension with the GQA paper.

922
00:54:07,060 --> 00:54:11,040
They show that GQA actually isn't that great.

923
00:54:17,680 --> 00:54:18,780
So this is MHA.

924
00:54:18,780 --> 00:54:19,640
This is GQA.

925
00:54:19,640 --> 00:54:23,140
These numbers are smaller than these numbers.

926
00:54:23,140 --> 00:54:29,640
But they show that their method, MLA, Multi-Latent Attention

927
00:54:29,640 --> 00:54:32,740
works even a little bit better than MHA.

928
00:54:32,740 --> 00:54:35,820
But let's just say it's about the same.

929
00:54:35,820 --> 00:54:39,360
So this column and this column are much better than—

930
00:54:39,360 --> 00:54:41,580
I guess there's no GQA on this table.

931
00:54:41,580 --> 00:54:43,740
You have to compare over here.

932
00:54:47,080 --> 00:54:50,600
So I'll show you an— yeah.

933
00:54:50,600 --> 00:54:54,160
How does this compare to reducing the number

934
00:54:54,160 --> 00:54:56,545
of dimensions, the model?

935
00:54:56,545 --> 00:54:57,920
So the question is, how does this

936
00:54:57,920 --> 00:55:02,160
compare with reducing the dimension of the model?

937
00:55:02,160 --> 00:55:03,880
So that's a good question.

938
00:55:03,880 --> 00:55:06,080
These ablations don't show that.

939
00:55:06,080 --> 00:55:08,280
My guess is that reducing the model dimension

940
00:55:08,280 --> 00:55:12,920
just makes things worse because you're indiscriminately

941
00:55:12,920 --> 00:55:15,440
just reducing everything.

942
00:55:15,440 --> 00:55:19,720
I think the trick in all of this kind of

943
00:55:19,720 --> 00:55:23,550
is to find places in the model where you can squeeze.

944
00:55:23,550 --> 00:55:25,710
And this apiary, I don't think you

945
00:55:25,710 --> 00:55:27,250
can necessarily know for sure.

946
00:55:27,250 --> 00:55:29,167
You just have to do a bunch of experimentation

947
00:55:29,167 --> 00:55:30,910
and see what works.

948
00:55:30,910 --> 00:55:34,470
So here's another idea to reduce your KV cache.

949
00:55:34,470 --> 00:55:37,510
This is called cross-layer attention.

950
00:55:37,510 --> 00:55:43,790
So the idea is that normally, every layer has KV, K's and V's.

951
00:55:43,790 --> 00:55:47,790
But let's say instead of doing that, we're just

952
00:55:47,790 --> 00:55:53,310
going to compute KVs for a subset of the layers.

953
00:55:53,310 --> 00:55:56,590
And then, just for this layer, I'm

954
00:55:56,590 --> 00:55:59,850
just going to use the previous layers on KV cache.

955
00:56:05,150 --> 00:56:07,010
So this is another way of sharing.

956
00:56:07,010 --> 00:56:11,030
Just like GQA shares K across heads,

957
00:56:11,030 --> 00:56:15,030
now I'm sharing KVs across layers.

958
00:56:15,030 --> 00:56:19,790
And empirically, this paper shows that doing this

959
00:56:19,790 --> 00:56:22,000
improves the Pareto frontier.

960
00:56:22,000 --> 00:56:28,380
所以这些模型中的每一个都更好——

961
00:56:28,380 --> 00:56:33,860
给定一种方法，你总是可以调整 KV

962
00:56:33,860 --> 00:56:41,060
缓存的大小，通过改变 K 和 head 维度，我猜这

963
00:56:41,060 --> 00:56:45,440
有点像你提到的改变模型维度的观点。

964
00:56:45,440 --> 00:56:53,700
但如果你做这种 CLA 跨层注意力，它会更好。

965
00:56:53,700 --> 00:56:59,180
那么继续，快速浏览不同的技术

966
00:56:59,180 --> 00:57:01,280
来减少 KV 缓存。

967
00:57:01,280 --> 00:57:04,620
有局部或滑动窗口注意力。

968
00:57:04,620 --> 00:57:06,900
这是一个相当古老的想法

969
00:57:06,900 --> 00:57:09,160
也是一个非常自然的想法。

970
00:57:09,160 --> 00:57:14,260
所以如果你看完整的注意力矩阵，它是 N 的平方。

971
00:57:14,260 --> 00:57:16,820
而不是那样做，如果你

972
00:57:16,820 --> 00:57:22,010
要生成一个 token，你只需看最后 K 个 token。

973
00:57:22,010 --> 00:57:24,210
所以你本质上有一个滑动窗口。

974
00:57:24,210 --> 00:57:29,610
对于你生成的每个 token，你只依赖于最后 K 个。

975
00:57:29,610 --> 00:57:34,970
现在，如果你这样做，有效的——

976
00:57:34,970 --> 00:57:37,950
所以 KV 缓存现在与序列长度无关。

977
00:57:37,950 --> 00:57:46,170
它只是批大小乘以其他变量，

978
00:57:46,170 --> 00:57:47,190
这很棒。

979
00:57:47,190 --> 00:57:51,330
这对于长上下文尤其棒。

980
00:57:51,330 --> 00:57:54,110
现在，由于层数，

981
00:57:54,110 --> 00:57:56,330
有效上下文长度实际上

982
00:57:56,330 --> 00:57:59,150
大于所声明的上下文长度

983
00:57:59,150 --> 00:58:03,570
因为信息可以传播得更远，如果

984
00:58:03,570 --> 00:58:05,890
你沿着层向下走。

985
00:58:05,890 --> 00:58:07,290
现在，你可以做更花哨的事情。

986
00:58:07,290 --> 00:58:11,950
你可以也许不做密集的层选择，

987
00:58:11,950 --> 00:58:13,750
但你可以把它间隔开。

988
00:58:13,750 --> 00:58:16,210
你也可以做这种全局加滑动窗口

989
00:58:16,210 --> 00:58:20,760
其中你有对固定的

990
00:58:20,760 --> 00:58:25,820
不同 token 点的网格的注意力，加上一个局部滑动窗口。

991
00:58:25,820 --> 00:58:27,760
所以你可以做各种事情。

992
00:58:27,760 --> 00:58:30,120
现在，这个的问题是它实际上

993
00:58:30,120 --> 00:58:32,440
仍然损害准确性。

994
00:58:32,440 --> 00:58:34,420
所以这降低了表达能力。

995
00:58:34,420 --> 00:58:38,520
这里没有免费的午餐，或者至少这

996
00:58:38,520 --> 00:58:41,600
是一顿昂贵的午餐。

997
00:58:41,600 --> 00:58:43,480
所以人们想出的解决方案

998
00:58:43,480 --> 00:58:47,700
是他们将局部注意力与全局注意力交错。

999
00:58:47,700 --> 00:58:52,880
所以这些混合模型对某些层有完整注意力

1000
00:58:52,880 --> 00:58:57,800
and some local layers for some of the other layers.

1001
00:58:57,800 --> 00:58:59,560
And you're basically always trying

1002
00:58:59,560 --> 00:59:04,360
to-- and then so that allows you to essentially reduce

1003
00:59:04,360 --> 00:59:05,940
the KV cache a little bit.

1004
00:59:05,940 --> 00:59:12,440
And you're always trying to balance accuracy with the speed.

1005
00:59:12,440 --> 00:59:13,340
Yeah.

1006
00:59:13,340 --> 00:59:15,256
Hybrid model-wise, is there a difference

1007
00:59:15,256 --> 00:59:18,110
between the trade-off in using a linear time invariant

1008
00:59:18,110 --> 00:59:19,530
versus a sliding window?

1009
00:59:19,530 --> 00:59:20,030
Yeah.

1010
00:59:20,030 --> 00:59:23,790
So the question is, what about linear attention versus sliding

1011
00:59:23,790 --> 00:59:24,930
window attention?

1012
00:59:24,930 --> 00:59:28,530
So I'm not going to talk about linear attention.

1013
00:59:28,530 --> 00:59:33,630
But very quickly, there's a bunch of methods where,

1014
00:59:33,630 --> 00:59:40,270
instead of storing KV cache, since you basically

1015
00:59:40,270 --> 00:59:45,370
compute some compressed representation of all the history.

1016
00:59:45,370 --> 00:59:47,790
So linear, the most naive linear attention

1017
00:59:47,790 --> 00:59:53,037
is you just sum the KV values up into a single vector.

1018
00:59:53,037 --> 00:59:55,370
So that's definitely independent of the sequence length.

1019
00:59:55,370 --> 00:59:56,490
You can do fancier things.

1020
00:59:56,490 --> 00:59:58,950
There's gated net, delta nets, and Mamba

1021
00:59:58,950 --> 01:00:04,630
which allows you to try to compress but not forget as much.

1022
01:00:04,630 --> 01:00:07,010
Now the question is, how do those compare?

1023
01:00:07,010 --> 01:00:09,550
Those have also been used in place

1024
01:00:09,550 --> 01:00:14,220
of sliding window attention, and people have gotten good results

1025
01:00:14,220 --> 01:00:14,880
with them.

1026
01:00:14,880 --> 01:00:17,480
You can also use a combination of full attention,

1027
