1267
01:13:19,510 --> 01:13:22,150
it's going to be pretty slow.

1268
01:13:22,150 --> 01:13:27,670
But if you are doing speculative decoding,

1269
01:13:27,670 --> 01:13:35,150
then you can see the small model generating a bunch of tokens,

1270
01:13:35,150 --> 01:13:42,230
and then the large model basically critiquing them.

1271
01:13:42,230 --> 01:13:44,990
And then, so you basically can get this burst

1272
01:13:44,990 --> 01:13:47,790
of tokens and then maybe another burst of tokens

1273
01:13:47,790 --> 01:13:48,970
and so on and so forth.

1274
01:13:51,820 --> 01:13:52,780
OK.

1275
01:13:52,780 --> 01:13:56,740
So here is the algorithm for-- there's

1276
01:13:56,740 --> 01:13:59,660
a few papers that came out with speculative decoding

1277
01:13:59,660 --> 01:14:01,280
around the same time.

1278
01:14:01,280 --> 01:14:04,380
This is one of them.

1279
01:14:04,380 --> 01:14:09,220
And so the idea here is that in order to generate,

1280
01:14:09,220 --> 01:14:11,952
we're going to generate K tokens.

1281
01:14:11,952 --> 01:14:14,160
And we're just going to sample from this draft model.

1282
01:14:14,160 --> 01:14:16,560
So p is the draft model.

1283
01:14:16,560 --> 01:14:18,500
We're going to sample K tokens.

1284
01:14:18,500 --> 01:14:20,380
And then in parallel, we're going

1285
01:14:20,380 --> 01:14:27,220
to compute the logits of these draft tokens using q.

1286
01:14:27,220 --> 01:14:31,040
And then now we have to determine whether we accept or not.

1287
01:14:31,040 --> 01:14:34,900
And this is where you do a bit of math and probability

1288
01:14:34,900 --> 01:14:36,060
and statistics.

1289
01:14:36,060 --> 01:14:41,420
You basically are going to accept with probability min 1

1290
01:14:41,420 --> 01:14:45,260
over this ratio of q over p.

1291
01:14:45,260 --> 01:14:52,630
So if q is much larger than p, the larger the q is,

1292
01:14:52,630 --> 01:14:54,390
the more likely you want to accept it.

1293
01:14:57,690 --> 01:15:03,290
And otherwise you sample from this residual distribution

1294
01:15:03,290 --> 01:15:04,770
and exit.

1295
01:15:04,770 --> 01:15:09,730
So this is basically rejection sampling

1296
01:15:09,730 --> 01:15:11,310
except for rejection sampling.

1297
01:15:11,310 --> 01:15:14,950
Sometimes when you reject you get nothing.

1298
01:15:14,950 --> 01:15:17,130
But here we always are guaranteed

1299
01:15:17,130 --> 01:15:20,610
to get an exact sample from the target model.

1300
01:15:20,610 --> 01:15:28,330
I'm going to skip this simple proof.

1301
01:15:28,330 --> 01:15:30,010
It's basically the same arguments

1302
01:15:30,010 --> 01:15:33,090
as rejection sampling to show that it's

1303
01:15:33,090 --> 01:15:40,850
the exact probabilities from the target model.

1304
01:15:40,850 --> 01:15:45,310
And the initial paper shows that this is fast.

1305
01:15:45,310 --> 01:15:50,330
And generally, if you have too few draft tokens,

1306
01:15:50,330 --> 01:15:54,590
you're not really leveraging the batching on the target model

1307
01:15:54,590 --> 01:15:55,210
side.

1308
01:15:55,210 --> 01:15:57,070
And if you have too many, then you're

1309
01:15:57,070 --> 01:15:58,650
going to reject more often.

1310
01:15:58,650 --> 01:16:04,470
So there's a sweet spot around, in this case, three or four.

1311
01:16:04,470 --> 01:16:07,590
And in general, the draft model is much smaller

1312
01:16:07,590 --> 01:16:08,890
than the target model.

1313
01:16:08,890 --> 01:16:11,670
And ideally, you want the draft model

1314
01:16:11,670 --> 01:16:13,510
to be as close to the target, which

1315
01:16:13,510 --> 01:16:17,550
means that you want to distill it,

1316
01:16:17,550 --> 01:16:20,670
which means that actually a lot of the same ideas that we just

1317
01:16:20,670 --> 01:16:24,510
talked about are applicable to speculative decoding as well.

1318
01:16:24,510 --> 01:16:28,030
So basically, the idea is that let's try

1319
01:16:28,030 --> 01:16:32,290
to reduce your KV cache via all the different shenanigans.

1320
01:16:32,290 --> 01:16:35,030
and if you end up with a model you're happy with,

1321
01:16:35,030 --> 01:16:36,090
just serve that.

1322
01:16:36,090 --> 01:16:37,910
if you're not happy with it, then

1323
01:16:37,910 --> 01:16:41,230
it at least can be a draft model, and you can use your main model

1324
01:16:41,230 --> 01:16:43,390
to fix things up.

1325
01:16:43,390 --> 01:16:45,580
there's a whole literature

1326
01:16:45,580 --> 01:16:48,100
on speculative decoding right now

1327
01:16:48,100 --> 01:16:55,780
that improve over the original, which I'll skip for now.

1328
01:16:55,780 --> 01:17:01,480
so very quickly now, dynamic workloads.

1329
01:17:01,480 --> 01:17:06,120
so this is the use case, is you're serving a live website,

1330
01:17:06,120 --> 01:17:09,060
and users come and chat with your model.

1331
01:17:09,060 --> 01:17:12,460
the requests arrive at different times.

1332
01:17:12,460 --> 01:17:14,600
they have different shared prefixes,

1333
01:17:14,600 --> 01:17:17,260
and they have different lengths.

1334
01:17:17,260 --> 01:17:19,820
so it's pretty messy.

1335
01:17:19,820 --> 01:17:23,020
it's far from this very simple training where

1336
01:17:23,020 --> 01:17:27,900
you have these blocks of the same number of tokens

1337
01:17:27,900 --> 01:17:29,140
all at once.

1338
01:17:29,140 --> 01:17:30,680
so what do you do in this case?

1339
01:17:30,680 --> 01:17:35,020
so there's this idea-- there's a system called

1340
01:17:35,020 --> 01:17:36,740
Orca that was built. this is actually

1341
01:17:36,740 --> 01:17:39,340
very early on which introduced this idea

1342
01:17:39,340 --> 01:17:41,340
of continuous batching.

1343
01:17:41,340 --> 01:17:45,770
so the idea is that you get a bunch of requests

1344
01:17:45,770 --> 01:17:46,710
that look like this.

1345
01:17:46,710 --> 01:17:49,290
so here's a prefix of the first request,

1346
01:17:49,290 --> 01:17:51,030
and you're generating this token.

1347
01:17:51,030 --> 01:17:52,448
here's the second one.

1348
01:17:52,448 --> 01:17:53,990
here's the third one, the fourth one.

1349
01:17:53,990 --> 01:17:56,930
it's jagged because every prefix has a different length.

1350
01:17:56,930 --> 01:17:58,570
and what we're going to do is we're

1351
01:17:58,570 --> 01:18:00,930
going to decode step by step.

1352
01:18:00,930 --> 01:18:05,710
so every step you decode one token for all the sequences.

1353
01:18:05,710 --> 01:18:08,510
next step, you decode another token for all the sequences.

1354
01:18:08,510 --> 01:18:13,850
and then if you end, you just eject that sequence.

1355
01:18:13,850 --> 01:18:16,730
and then as new requests arrive to the batch,

1356
01:18:16,730 --> 01:18:19,730
then you basically put it in the batch,

1357
01:18:19,730 --> 01:18:22,913
and then you just continue.

1358
01:18:22,913 --> 01:18:25,330
so that's why it's called continuous batching because it's

1359
01:18:25,330 --> 01:18:29,370
this batch is dynamically being updated

1360
01:18:29,370 --> 01:18:32,970
with either old finished sequences being evicted

1361
01:18:32,970 --> 01:18:34,770
and new ones coming in.

1362
01:18:34,770 --> 01:18:39,250
So now, one problem here is that everything

1363
01:18:39,250 --> 01:18:41,760
we've seen batching works when all the sequences have

1364
01:18:41,760 --> 01:18:43,160
the same dimensionality.

1365
01:18:43,160 --> 01:18:44,600
You have tensors.

1366
01:18:44,600 --> 01:18:46,960
Every slice has the same dimensionality.

1367
01:18:46,960 --> 01:18:50,260
But if your request here has a different length,

1368
01:18:50,260 --> 01:18:51,840
so what do you do about that?

1369
01:18:51,840 --> 01:18:54,200
So there's this idea called selective batching

1370
01:18:54,200 --> 01:19:00,640
where let's say you have a length 3, length 9, and a length

1371
01:19:00,640 --> 01:19:01,680
5.

1372
01:19:01,680 --> 01:19:04,820
So here in the attention computation,

1373
01:19:04,820 --> 01:19:06,560
you can't really do anything about this

1374
01:19:06,560 --> 01:19:11,940
because attention depends on the length of your sequence.

1375
01:19:11,940 --> 01:19:16,140
So if you have a 3 by 3, a 9 by 9 computation,

1376
01:19:16,140 --> 01:19:21,320
you can't really share the tensor effectively.

1377
01:19:21,320 --> 01:19:23,780
But for the non-attention, the MLP layers,

1378
01:19:23,780 --> 01:19:28,160
which take up a lot of FLOPs, you can actually just

1379
01:19:28,160 --> 01:19:30,680
concatenate all the sequences together

1380
01:19:30,680 --> 01:19:34,720
to form a mega sequence, and you process that.

1381
01:19:34,720 --> 01:19:38,640
So the final idea is page attention.

1382
01:19:38,640 --> 01:19:41,910
This was introduced in the PagedAttention paper.

1383
01:19:41,910 --> 01:19:44,250
Of course, PagedAttention has many other bells and whistles now,

1384
01:19:44,250 --> 01:19:49,030
but this is the core idea at that time.

1385
01:19:49,030 --> 01:19:54,830
So the question is, how is the KV cache stored?

1386
01:19:54,830 --> 01:20:00,260
So if you think about requests coming in,

1387
01:20:00,260 --> 01:20:03,010
you have to put them in memory somewhere.

1388
01:20:03,010 --> 01:20:04,750
And in general, there's this problem

1389
01:20:04,750 --> 01:20:06,250
that you get fragmentation.

1390
01:20:06,250 --> 01:20:08,070
This is what happens to--

1391
01:20:08,070 --> 01:20:11,310
or used to happen to your hard drive.

1392
01:20:11,310 --> 01:20:15,630
And you have to defrag your hard drive back in the day.

1393
01:20:15,630 --> 01:20:19,730
So there's two types of fragmentation.

1394
01:20:19,730 --> 01:20:23,910
One is that you have to allocate enough buffer so

1395
01:20:23,910 --> 01:20:26,910
that-- because you don't know when you're going to stop.

1396
01:20:26,910 --> 01:20:31,030
So you might have a max token limit of 1,024.

1397
01:20:31,030 --> 01:20:32,770
So you have to allocate all this memory.

1398
01:20:32,770 --> 01:20:34,228
And you can't put anything in there

1399
01:20:34,228 --> 01:20:36,150
because you're going to just generate

1400
01:20:36,150 --> 01:20:39,720
until you hit the max tokens, and that's very wasteful.

1401
01:20:39,720 --> 01:20:41,380
That's internal fragmentation.

1402
01:20:41,380 --> 01:20:44,460
And then there's also, you have-- there could be space

1403
01:20:44,460 --> 01:20:46,360
between different requests.

1404
01:20:46,360 --> 01:20:50,040
And that space is maybe too small to get used effectively.

1405
01:20:50,040 --> 01:20:52,660
So that's just wasted space.

1406
01:20:52,660 --> 01:20:56,738
So the solution here is, these are systems people.

1407
01:20:56,738 --> 01:20:58,280
So they know their operating systems.

1408
01:20:58,280 --> 01:21:01,380
They said, OK, well we've solved this problem once before,

1409
01:21:01,380 --> 01:21:04,180
so let's just use the same idea here.

1410
01:21:04,180 --> 01:21:06,780
So we're going to divide the KV cache of a sequence

1411
01:21:06,780 --> 01:21:11,440
into non-contiguous blocks, OK?

1412
01:21:11,440 --> 01:21:14,780
So if you have this sequence, four score and seven years

1413
01:21:14,780 --> 01:21:16,500
ago our fathers brought forth, we're

1414
01:21:16,500 --> 01:21:19,460
just going to chunk it up into these blocks of size 4.

1415
01:21:19,460 --> 01:21:21,860
Doesn't matter where the blocks go, but they're

1416
01:21:21,860 --> 01:21:25,080
going to be aligned according to the block.

1417
01:21:25,080 --> 01:21:27,780
So there's some uniformity there.

1418
01:21:27,780 --> 01:21:32,980
So when two requests share the same--

1419
01:21:32,980 --> 01:21:35,080
can actually share the same KV cache.

1420
01:21:40,410 --> 01:21:45,130
So you might have this block and this block.

1421
01:21:45,130 --> 01:21:48,170
So this block might go here and here,

1422
01:21:48,170 --> 01:21:49,870
and this block might be over there.

1423
01:21:49,870 --> 01:21:52,710
So they're interspersed.

1424
01:21:52,710 --> 01:21:54,310
But as long as you have the indices

1425
01:21:54,310 --> 01:21:58,410
and keep track of where everything is, it's fine.

1426
01:21:58,410 --> 01:22:05,330
So in particular, if you have system prompts, then

1427
01:22:05,330 --> 01:22:09,890
you can actually just cache these system prompts,

1428
01:22:09,890 --> 01:22:12,330
the KV cache and the system prompts, once.

1429
01:22:12,330 --> 01:22:15,710
And that can be useful for all the queries, OK?

1430
01:22:15,710 --> 01:22:17,810
So this is very useful because if a lot of people

1431
01:22:17,810 --> 01:22:19,185
are using the same system prompt,

1432
01:22:19,185 --> 01:22:24,450
then you don't have to compute the cache for every request.

1433
01:22:24,450 --> 01:22:29,090
Also there are many applications where you have the same prompt,

1434
01:22:29,090 --> 01:22:31,650
and you actually want to generate multiple responses.

1435
01:22:31,650 --> 01:22:34,550
So in that case, you can also just share

1436
01:22:34,550 --> 01:22:39,070
the KV cache for the prompt and just

1437
01:22:39,070 --> 01:22:42,670
have unique responses coming out.

1438
01:22:42,670 --> 01:22:46,990
OK, so for example, if you were to generate,

1439
01:22:46,990 --> 01:22:50,150
let's say, multiple generations from score

1440
01:22:50,150 --> 01:22:55,550
and seven years ago are, blank, and so what would happen here

1441
01:22:55,550 --> 01:22:57,950
is that you would have score and seven.

1442
01:22:57,950 --> 01:23:02,750
And you would start by having years ago or our.

1443
01:23:02,750 --> 01:23:07,170
And then this is called copy on write semantics.

1444
01:23:07,170 --> 01:23:08,130
You keep this.

1445
01:23:08,130 --> 01:23:10,290
And then we have these two samples.

1446
01:23:10,290 --> 01:23:14,510
And if they had happened to sample let's say the same token,

1447
01:23:14,510 --> 01:23:17,010
you just continue with that.

1448
01:23:17,010 --> 01:23:19,670
But if they sample different tokens, you split the block,

1449
01:23:19,670 --> 01:23:22,030
and then you can continue there.

1450
01:23:22,030 --> 01:23:25,110
So you're basically sharing as much of the prefix cache

1451
01:23:25,110 --> 01:23:28,310
as you can.

1452
01:23:28,310 --> 01:23:33,060
There's a bunch of other optimizations like kernels

1453
01:23:33,060 --> 01:23:36,900
that I'm not going to have time to go over.

1454
01:23:36,900 --> 01:23:39,140
But the general idea is that you're

1455
01:23:39,140 --> 01:23:44,420
using these operating systems metaphors to manage

1456
01:23:44,420 --> 01:23:46,460
your inference.

1457
01:23:46,460 --> 01:23:51,740
So summary here-- inference is really, really important.

1458
01:23:51,740 --> 01:23:54,220
It's very different from training.

1459
01:23:54,220 --> 01:23:57,060
Even though it's the same model, but you're asking the model

1460
01:23:57,060 --> 01:24:00,500
to do something very different, ends up being very memory-bound.

1461
01:24:00,500 --> 01:24:06,820
And it's also dynamic if you're in a live chatbot use case.

1462
01:24:06,820 --> 01:24:11,560
We saw a variety of different techniques to improve inference.

1463
01:24:11,560 --> 01:24:12,360
You can quantize.

1464
01:24:12,360 --> 01:24:14,440
You can come up with new architectures.

1465
01:24:14,440 --> 01:24:16,280
You can prune and distill.

1466
01:24:16,280 --> 01:24:19,380
You can also use speculative sampling.

1467
01:24:19,380 --> 01:24:22,380
But all of these are driven by the same principle

1468
01:24:22,380 --> 01:24:24,940
here, which is reduce your KV cache

1469
01:24:24,940 --> 01:24:28,540
but don't hurt accuracy too much.

1470
01:24:28,540 --> 01:24:33,730
And then there's ideas from systems like paging

1471
01:24:33,730 --> 01:24:35,850
and speculative execution that can

1472
01:24:35,850 --> 01:24:40,420
be brought to bear for actually live inference servers.

1473
01:24:40,420 --> 01:24:42,670
One thing we didn't really get a chance to talk about,

1474
01:24:42,670 --> 01:24:44,730
which was discussed briefly, is that I

1475
01:24:44,730 --> 01:24:48,210
think that new architectures have actually a huge potential

1476
01:24:48,210 --> 01:24:51,410
for improvement, things like state space models

1477
01:24:51,410 --> 01:24:54,810
or linear attention or diffusion.

1478
01:24:54,810 --> 01:24:58,330
At some level, the KV cache and the way

1479
01:24:58,330 --> 01:25:01,130
that attention is built fundamentally

1480
01:25:01,130 --> 01:25:06,010
makes it an inference-unfriendly kind of architecture.

1481
01:25:06,010 --> 01:25:08,690
So if you can come up with a new architecture that

1482
01:25:08,690 --> 01:25:10,810
is designed for inference in the way

1483
01:25:10,810 --> 01:25:15,330
that the transformer was not, this can maybe unlock a lot.

1484
01:25:15,330 --> 01:25:18,090
OK, so I will stop there.

1485
01:25:18,090 --> 01:25:21,210
And next class, Tatsu will return and talk

1486
01:25:21,210 --> 01:25:24,280
about scaling laws part 2.
